regate-error","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/aggregate-error#related","content":"Related"},"8":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-6","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/aggregate-error#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"9":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-7","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#aggregateerror","content":"`AggregateError` is a built-in error whose own `errors` property carries the list of individual failures one operation collected."},"10":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-8","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#what-it-does","content":"The constructor takes `errors` and an optional `message`, converts the first argument with `IterableToList`, and installs the resulting array as a non-enumerable, writable, configurable own `errors` property. `IterableToList` walks the argument through its iterator protocol, so any iterable works and a non-iterable throws. The constructor is callable without `new`, in which case the active function object stands in for `NewTarget`. `AggregateError.prototype.name` is `\"AggregateError\"` and `AggregateError.prototype.message` is the empty string, so an omitted message leaves nothing on the instance. Instances carry the `[[ErrorData]]` slot that `Object.prototype.toString` uses to recognise an error. The proposal chose an error type over a plain array so that everything a built-in throws stays an `Error` instance and carries a stack trace."},"11":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-9","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#gotchas","content":"Serialisation drops the list, because `errors` is defined as non-enumerable: `Object.keys(error)` is empty and `JSON.stringify(error)` yields `{}`, so a log pipeline has to read `error.errors` explicitly. The property holds a copy, since `IterableToList` drains the iterable into a new List, so later pushes to the original array are invisible and an exhausted iterator contributes nothing. The first argument is required in practice: `new AggregateError()` throws a `TypeError` on the missing iterable while the message stays optional. `Object.prototype.toString.call` reports `[object Error]` rather than a distinct tag, because the recognised slot is `[[ErrorData]]`, so `name` is what separates it from a plain `Error`."},"12":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-10","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#support","content":"V8 shipped the constructor alongside its first consumer in 8.5, the release that also added the combinator which rejects with it."},"13":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-11","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#related","content":"promise-any"},"14":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-12","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#related","content":"error-cause"},"15":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-13","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Promise.any + AggregateError § why-throw-an-aggregateerror-instead-of-an-array"},"16":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-14","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"17":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-15","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: AggregateError ( errors, message )"},"18":{"id":"/features/aggregate-error-16","page_id":"/features/aggregate-error","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/aggregate-error#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},