eatures/array-flat-flat-map-2","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"62":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-3","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"63":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-4","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#support","content":"Support"},"64":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-5","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#related","content":"Related"},"65":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-6","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"66":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-7","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#arrayprototypeflat-and-flatmap","content":"`flat` copies an array with nested arrays spliced in to a chosen depth, and `flatMap` maps each element before splicing one level."},"67":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-8","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#what-it-does","content":"`flat` reads `length`, sets its depth to `1` unless the argument is present, in which case it takes `ToInteger(depth)`, creates the result with `ArraySpeciesCreate(O, 0)`, and delegates to `FlattenIntoArray`. `flatMap` throws a `TypeError` when its first argument is not callable and calls the same operation with a fixed depth of `1` plus the mapper and `thisArg`. `FlattenIntoArray` visits each index, skips one whose `HasProperty` check fails, applies the mapper when present, and recurses only while the remaining depth is above zero and `IsArray` holds for the element; otherwise it writes the element with `CreateDataPropertyOrThrow` and throws a `TypeError` past index 2^53 − 1. `Array.prototype[@@unscopables]` gains `flat` and `flatMap` entries."},"68":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-9","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#gotchas","content":"`flat(undefined)` flattens one level while `flat(null)` and `flat(0)` only copy, because the depth default applies to an absent or `undefined` argument and every other value goes through `ToInteger`. Only values for which `IsArray` is true are spliced, so `[[1], \"ab\", new Set([2])].flat()` keeps the string and the set intact. `flatMap` always flattens exactly one level and ignores any depth argument, so a mapper returning `[[value]]` yields nested arrays. Holes vanish rather than becoming `undefined`, since the walk skips an index whose `HasProperty` check fails. Both names are listed in `@@unscopables`, so a `with` block over an array does not resolve `flat` or `flatMap` to the methods."},"69":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-10","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#support","content":"V8 shipped both methods in 6.9, whose examples give `flat` a default depth of `1` and `flat(Infinity)` for full recursion."},"70":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-11","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#related","content":"iterator-helpers"},"71":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-12","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Array.prototype.\\{flat,flatMap}"},"72":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-13","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"73":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-14","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Array.prototype.flat ( \\[ depth ] )"},"74":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-15","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"75":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-16","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Array.prototype.flatMap ( mapperFunction \\[ , thisArg ] )"},"76":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-17","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"77":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-18","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: FlattenIntoArray"},"78":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-19","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"79":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-20","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Array.prototype \\[ @@unscopables ]"},"80":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-21","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"81":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-22","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Array.prototype.\\{flat,flatMap} for Stage 4"},"82":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-23","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-01-29, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"83":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-24","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"V8 release v6.9: JavaScript language features"},"84":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-25","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2018-08-07, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"85":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-26","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 117th General Assembly"},"86":{"id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map-27","page_id":"/features/array-flat-flat-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-flat-flat-map#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"87":{"id":"/features/array-from-async","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"page","content":"Array.fromAsync","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2026"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async"},"88":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-0","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async#arrayfromasync","content":"Array.fromAsync"},"89":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-1","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"90":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-2","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"91":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-3","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"92":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-4","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"93":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-5","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#arrayfromasync","content":"`Array.fromAsync` collects async iterables, sync iterables, or array-like inputs into a promised array."},"94":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-6","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#what-it-does","content":"The method returns a promise and consumes an async iterator when available. For a sync iterable, it awaits each yielded value; for an array-like input, it awaits each indexed value. An optional mapping callback runs on each awaited value before insertion."},"95":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-7","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#gotchas","content":"Consumption is sequential, matching `for await...of`; the method does not await all input promises concurrently. Errors from iteration, awaiting, mapping, or element definition reject the returned promise and trigger the specified iterator closing path."},"96":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-8","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Array.fromAsync for JavaScript § description"},"97":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-9","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"98":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-10","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Array.fromAsync for JavaScript § errors"},"99":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-11","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"100":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-12","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2026: Array.fromAsync"},"101":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-13","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2026-06-30, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"102":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-14","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Array.fromAsync for Stage 4"},"103":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-15","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2025-05-28, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"104":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-16","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"105":{"id":"/features/array-from-async-17","page_id":"/features/array-from-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-from-async#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2026-07-01, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"106":{"id":"/features/array-grouping","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"page","content":"Object.groupBy and Map.groupBy","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2024"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping"},"107":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-0","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping#objectgroupby-and-mapgroupby","content":"Object.groupBy and Map.groupBy"},"108":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-1","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"109":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-2","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"110":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-3","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"111":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-4","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"112":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-5","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#objectgroupby-and-mapgroupby","content":"The grouping methods partition iterable values by keys returned from a callback."},"113":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-6","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#what-it-does","content":"`Object.groupBy()` returns a null-prototype object whose property keys map to arrays. `Map.groupBy()` returns a `Map` and preserves arbitrary key values by identity. Both pass each element and its zero-based index to the callback."},"114":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-7","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#gotchas","content":"`Object.groupBy()` converts callback results to property keys, while `Map.groupBy()` does not. Consumers must account for absent groups because only keys produced by the callback appear in the result. The specification creates groups only as callback keys are encountered."},"115":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-8","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Array grouping proposal"},"116":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-9","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"117":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-10","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Array grouping proposal § motivation"},"118":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-11","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"119":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-12","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2024: Object.groupBy"},"120":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-13","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2024-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"121":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-14","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Array grouping for Stage 4"},"122":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-15","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2023-11-27, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"123":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-16","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"124":{"id":"/features/array-grouping-17","page_id":"/features/array-grouping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-grouping#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2024-07-03, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"125":{"id":"/features/array-includes","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"page","content":"Array.prototype.includes","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2016"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes"},"126":{"id":"/features/array-includes-0","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#arrayprototypeincludes","content":"Array.prototype.includes"},"127":{"id":"/features/array-includes-1","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"128":{"id":"/features/array-includes-2","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"129":{"id":"/features/array-includes-3","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"130":{"id":"/features/array-includes-4","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#support","content":"Support"},"131":{"id":"/features/array-includes-5","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#related","content":"Related"},"132":{"id":"/features/array-includes-6","page_id":"/features/array-includes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"133":{"id":"/features/array-includes-7","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#arrayprototypeincludes","content":"A method reports whether an array holds a value, comparing with SameValueZero so a search for `NaN` succeeds."},"134":{"id":"/features/array-includes-8","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#what-it-does","content":"`Array.prototype.includes(searchElement, fromIndex)` walks indices in ascending order, compares each element to `searchElement` with SameValueZero, and returns `true` at the first match or `false` after the last index. `fromIndex` defaults to 0, counts from the end when negative, clamps to 0 when the computed index is below 0, and yields `false` without a search when it reaches the length. SameValueZero differs from strict equality by treating `NaN` as equal to itself, and from `SameValue` by treating `+0` and `-0` as equal."},"135":{"id":"/features/array-includes-9","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#what-it-does","content":"The proposal's stated targets are the `indexOf(el) !== -1` idiom, which asks for a position in order to answer a membership question, and that idiom's failure on `NaN`. It keeps `fromIndex` for consistency with `Array.prototype.indexOf` and `String.prototype.includes`. The same proposal installs the method on `%TypedArray%.prototype`, as it does for every non-mutating array method, and `%TypedArray%.prototype.includes` runs the same algorithm against the `[[ArrayLength]]` slot instead of a `length` property read, after `ValidateTypedArray` rejects a receiver that is not a typed array."},"136":{"id":"/features/array-includes-10","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#gotchas","content":"Holes count as `undefined` members. The algorithm reads every index with `Get` rather than skipping absent ones, so a sparse array reports a value `indexOf` never finds:"},"137":{"id":"/features/array-includes-11","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#gotchas","content":"Signed zero collapses: `[-0].includes(0)` is `true`, because SameValueZero compares the two zeroes as equal, which is the same reason `Map` and `Set` treat them as one key. The proposal names that as a refactoring hazard when replacing `indexOf`, since the two methods disagree on arrays containing `NaN`."},"138":{"id":"/features/array-includes-12","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#gotchas","content":"A negative `fromIndex` shortens the search instead of restarting it: `[1, 2, 3].includes(1, -1)` is `false` because the offset lands on the last index. `includes` is also added to the `Array.prototype [ @@unscopables ]` blacklist, so inside a `with` block over an array the name resolves to an outer binding rather than to the method."},"139":{"id":"/features/array-includes-13","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#support","content":"V8 4.7 lists the method under upcoming ES features and describes it as returning a boolean for membership, while the proposal was still at Stage 3."},"140":{"id":"/features/array-includes-14","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#related","content":"iterator-includes"},"141":{"id":"/features/array-includes-15","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Array.prototype.includes proposal: Motivation"},"142":{"id":"/features/array-includes-16","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"143":{"id":"/features/array-includes-17","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Array.prototype.includes proposal: Proposed Solution"},"144":{"id":"/features/array-includes-18","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"145":{"id":"/features/array-includes-19","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Array.prototype.includes proposal: Why SameValueZero?"},"146":{"id":"/features/array-includes-20","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"147":{"id":"/features/array-includes-21","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Array.prototype.includes proposal: Typed Arrays"},"148":{"id":"/features/array-includes-22","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"149":{"id":"/features/array-includes-23","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Array.prototype.includes proposal: Status"},"150":{"id":"/features/array-includes-24","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"151":{"id":"/features/array-includes-25","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2016: Array.prototype.includes ( searchElement \\[ , fromIndex ] )"},"152":{"id":"/features/array-includes-26","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2016-06-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"153":{"id":"/features/array-includes-27","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2016: %TypedArray%.prototype.includes ( searchElement \\[ , fromIndex ] )"},"154":{"id":"/features/array-includes-28","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2016-06-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"155":{"id":"/features/array-includes-29","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2016: SameValueZero ( x, y )"},"156":{"id":"/features/array-includes-30","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2016-06-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"157":{"id":"/features/array-includes-31","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2016: Array.prototype \\[ @@unscopables ]"},"158":{"id":"/features/array-includes-32","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2016-06-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"159":{"id":"/features/array-includes-33","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Array.prototype.includes"},"160":{"id":"/features/array-includes-34","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2015-11-17, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"161":{"id":"/features/array-includes-35","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"V8 release v4.7: Array.prototype.includes"},"162":{"id":"/features/array-includes-36","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2015-10-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"163":{"id":"/features/array-includes-37","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Last results of the 111th General Assembly held in Montreux, June 14, 2016"},"164":{"id":"/features/array-includes-38","page_id":"/features/array-includes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/array-includes#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2016-06-14, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"165":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"page","content":"ArrayBuffer transfer","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2024"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer"},"166":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-0","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#arraybuffer-transfer","content":"ArrayBuffer transfer"},"167":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-1","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"168":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-2","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"169":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-3","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"170":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-4","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#related","content":"Related"},"171":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-5","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"172":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-6","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#arraybuffer-transfer","content":"Array buffers can move their bytes into a new buffer while detaching the original."},"173":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-7","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#what-it-does","content":"`transfer()` preserves resizability, while `transferToFixedLength()` returns a fixed-length buffer. Both accept an optional new byte length, preserve the shared byte prefix, zero new memory, and detach the source. The `detached` getter reports whether an `ArrayBuffer` has been detached."},"174":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-8","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#gotchas","content":"Views over the source buffer become unusable after transfer. A requested size outside the source buffer's permitted range throws, and transferring a `SharedArrayBuffer` is not supported by these methods. The standardized operations apply only to `ArrayBuffer` instances and validate the new length."},"175":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-9","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#related","content":"resizable-arraybuffers"},"176":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-10","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"ArrayBuffer transfer proposal § api"},"177":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-11","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"178":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-12","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2024: ArrayBuffer objects"},"179":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-13","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2024-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"180":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-14","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"TC39 notes: ArrayBuffer transfer for Stage 4"},"181":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-15","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2024-02-06, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"182":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-16","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"183":{"id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer-17","page_id":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/arraybuffer-transfer#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2024-07-03, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"184":{"id":"/features/async-functions","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"page","content":"Async functions","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2017"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions"},"185":{"id":"/features/async-functions-0","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#async-functions","content":"Async functions"},"186":{"id":"/features/async-functions-1","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"187":{"id":"/features/async-functions-2","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"188":{"id":"/features/async-functions-3","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"189":{"id":"/features/async-functions-4","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#support","content":"Support"},"190":{"id":"/features/async-functions-5","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#related","content":"Related"},"191":{"id":"/features/async-functions-6","page_id":"/features/async-functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"192":{"id":"/features/async-functions-7","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#async-functions","content":"The `async` keyword marks a function whose body can suspend on `await` and whose call returns a promise."},"193":{"id":"/features/async-functions-8","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#what-it-does","content":"The grammar adds `AsyncFunctionDeclaration`, `AsyncFunctionExpression`, and `AsyncMethod`, each written as `async` followed with no line break by `function` or by a property name. Calling one creates a promise capability before the body runs, starts the body, and returns that capability's promise, so a `return` fulfills it and a thrown exception rejects it. `await` resolves its operand through a new promise capability and registers resumption functions that restore the saved execution context, which is how the suspended body continues with the fulfillment value or resumes with a throw completion."},"194":{"id":"/features/async-functions-9","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#what-it-does","content":"An `AsyncFunction` constructor is specified as a subclass of `Function` and is reachable only through the prototype of an async function, not as a global binding. Instances have `[[FunctionKind]]` `\"async\"`, carry no `[[Construct]]` method, and have no `prototype` property."},"195":{"id":"/features/async-functions-10","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#what-it-does","content":"The proposal states its purpose as replacing the promise-and-generator model of asynchronous code with a language-level one."},"196":{"id":"/features/async-functions-11","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#gotchas","content":"`async` is not a reserved word and `await` is only an operator inside an async body. The declaration form requires `async` with no line terminator before `function`, so a newline between them parses `async` as an identifier reference instead. `function f() { var await = 1; }` stays legal, while the same body inside `async function` is a `SyntaxError`, and `const async = 5` is still a valid declaration."},"197":{"id":"/features/async-functions-12","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#gotchas","content":"An async function is callable but not constructable. Instances have no `[[Construct]]` method and no `prototype` property, so `new (async function () {})()` throws a `TypeError` and `(async function () {}).prototype` is `undefined`. The constructor is reached with `Object.getPrototypeOf(async function () {}).constructor`, whose `name` is `AsyncFunction`, and `Object.prototype.toString` reports `[object AsyncFunction]`."},"198":{"id":"/features/async-functions-13","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#gotchas","content":"A body always yields a promise, even with no `await` in it, because the promise capability is created before the body runs. Awaiting a plain value still suspends, since `await` resolves its operand through a promise capability rather than reading it directly."},"199":{"id":"/features/async-functions-14","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#support","content":"V8 shipped async functions in 5.5, which describes waiting for a promise as writing `await` in front of it with no callback."},"200":{"id":"/features/async-functions-15","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#related","content":"async-iteration"},"201":{"id":"/features/async-functions-16","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#related","content":"top-level-await"},"202":{"id":"/features/async-functions-17","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Async Functions for ECMAScript"},"203":{"id":"/features/async-functions-18","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"204":{"id":"/features/async-functions-19","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: Async Function Definitions"},"205":{"id":"/features/async-functions-20","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"206":{"id":"/features/async-functions-21","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: Async Function Definitions, Runtime Semantics: EvaluateBody"},"207":{"id":"/features/async-functions-22","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"208":{"id":"/features/async-functions-23","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: AsyncFunctionAwait ( value )"},"209":{"id":"/features/async-functions-24","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"210":{"id":"/features/async-functions-25","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: The AsyncFunction Constructor"},"211":{"id":"/features/async-functions-26","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"212":{"id":"/features/async-functions-27","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: AsyncFunction Instances"},"213":{"id":"/features/async-functions-28","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"214":{"id":"/features/async-functions-29","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Async functions"},"215":{"id":"/features/async-functions-30","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2016-07-28, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"216":{"id":"/features/async-functions-31","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"V8 release v5.5: Async functions"},"217":{"id":"/features/async-functions-32","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2016-10-24, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"218":{"id":"/features/async-functions-33","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Last results of the 113th Ecma General Assembly held in Montreux, June 27, 2017"},"219":{"id":"/features/async-functions-34","page_id":"/features/async-functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-functions#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"220":{"id":"/features/async-iteration","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"page","content":"Asynchronous iteration","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2018"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration"},"221":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-0","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#asynchronous-iteration","content":"Asynchronous iteration"},"222":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-1","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"223":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-2","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"224":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-3","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"225":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-4","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#support","content":"Support"},"226":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-5","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#related","content":"Related"},"227":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-6","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"228":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-7","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#asynchronous-iteration","content":"`Symbol.asyncIterator`, `for await (… of …)`, and `async function*` together iterate a sequence whose completion is only known asynchronously."},"229":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-8","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#what-it-does","content":"`IterationStatement` gains `for await ( … of AssignmentExpression ) Statement` beside the `for-in` and `for-of` forms. `Symbol.asyncIterator` is the well-known symbol an object publishes to advertise the protocol, non-writable and non-configurable like the other well-known symbols. An AsyncIterator's `next` returns a promise for an iterator result, and once a returned promise fulfils with `done: true` every later call must do the same. `async function*` declarations, methods, and expressions produce async generators, and an Async-from-Sync Iterator object adapts a plain synchronous iterator when the operand publishes no `Symbol.asyncIterator`."},"230":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-9","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#what-it-does","content":"The proposal's argument is that a synchronous iterator must know both the next value and the done state by the time `next` returns, and that an iterator of promises does not fix this because only the value becomes asynchronous. It therefore returns one promise for the whole `{ value, done }` pair, and queues method calls internally so a second `next` before the first settles still resolves in order. Async generators differ from generators in three named ways: their `next`, `throw`, and `return` return promises, `await` and `for await` are allowed in the body, and `yield*` delegates to async iterables."},"231":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-10","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#gotchas","content":"`for await` accepts a synchronous iterable too, and then awaits each yielded value: `for await (const value of [Promise.resolve(\"a\"), \"b\"])` produces `\"a\"` and `\"b\"` rather than a promise and a string, because the statement falls back to an Async-from-Sync Iterator object. That fallback makes the loop silently accept an operand that publishes only `Symbol.iterator`."},"232":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-11","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#gotchas","content":"The statement is restricted to contexts that can await: the grammar places `for await` in the `IterationStatement` production, and the proposal states that async `for-of` is allowed only inside async functions and async generator functions. Iteration is also strictly sequential: the proposal states that each access to the next value implicitly awaits the promise the iterator method returned, so an async generator over network calls issues them one at a time."},"233":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-12","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#support","content":"V8 shipped async iterators and generators in 6.3, which lists them beside `Promise.prototype.finally` and dynamic `import()` as the Stage 3 features of that release."},"234":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-13","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#related","content":"Array.fromAsync drains an async iterable into an array with the same sequential awaiting this protocol defines."},"235":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-14","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#related","content":"Explicit resource management adds `await using`, which disposes asynchronously inside a `for await` body without changing how the loop iterates."},"236":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-15","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Asynchronous Iterators for JavaScript: Overview and motivation"},"237":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-16","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"238":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-17","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Asynchronous Iterators: Async iterators and async iterables"},"239":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-18","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"240":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-19","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Asynchronous Iterators: The async iteration statement"},"241":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-20","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"242":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-21","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Asynchronous Iterators: Async generator functions"},"243":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-22","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"244":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-23","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: The for-in, for-of, and for-await-of Statements"},"245":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-24","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"246":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-25","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Symbol.asyncIterator"},"247":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-26","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"248":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-27","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: The AsyncIterator Interface"},"249":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-28","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"250":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-29","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: AsyncGeneratorFunction Definitions"},"251":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-30","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"252":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-31","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Async-from-Sync Iterator Objects"},"253":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-32","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"254":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-33","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Async Iteration for stage 4"},"255":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-34","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-01-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"256":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-35","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Async iteration for stage 4, deferred"},"257":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-36","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-01-23, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"258":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-37","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"V8 release v6.3: JavaScript language features"},"259":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-38","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2017-10-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"260":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-39","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Results of the 115th Ecma General Assembly held in Geneva, June 27, 2018"},"261":{"id":"/features/async-iteration-40","page_id":"/features/async-iteration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/async-iteration#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"262":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"page","content":"Atomics.pause","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2027"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause"},"263":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-0","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause#atomicspause","content":"Atomics.pause"},"264":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-1","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"265":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-2","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"266":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-3","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"267":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-4","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"268":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-5","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#atomicspause","content":"`Atomics.pause` supplies an implementation-defined, finite CPU pause for spin-wait loops."},"269":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-6","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#what-it-does","content":"The zero-argument method lets an engine issue an architecture-appropriate processor hint while JavaScript polls shared state. The specification sends the spin-wait signal, if the environment supports one, and returns `undefined`. It can run on main or worker threads because it does not block like `Atomics.wait`."},"270":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-7","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#gotchas","content":"The pause duration is not observable except through timing, and the call does not transfer execution to another thread. An earlier duration parameter was removed because implementations did not vary behavior from it."},"271":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-8","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Micro waits in JS § atomicspause"},"272":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-9","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"273":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-10","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Micro waits in JS § does-atomicspause-yield-execution-to-another-thread"},"274":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-11","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"275":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-12","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Micro waits in JS § why-doesnt-atomicspause-take-an-optional-argument"},"276":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-13","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"277":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-14","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Atomics.pause proposal specification text"},"278":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-15","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"279":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-16","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 finished proposals at the cutoff"},"280":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-17","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"281":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-18","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Atomics.pause for Stage 4"},"282":{"id":"/features/atomics-pause-19","page_id":"/features/atomics-pause","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-pause#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2026-05-19, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"283":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"page","content":"Atomics.waitAsync","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2024"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async"},"284":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-0","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#atomicswaitasync","content":"Atomics.waitAsync"},"285":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-1","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"286":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-2","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"287":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-3","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"288":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-4","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"289":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-5","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#atomicswaitasync","content":"`Atomics.waitAsync()` waits for a shared-memory notification without blocking the calling agent."},"290":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-6","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#what-it-does","content":"The method returns `{ async, value }`. A failed initial comparison or immediate timeout returns a string synchronously; an actual wait returns a promise that fulfills with `\"ok\"` or `\"timed-out\"`. `Atomics.notify()` wakes synchronous and asynchronous waiters through the same wait queue."},"291":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-7","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#gotchas","content":"The outer result is not itself a promise. Callers must inspect `async` before awaiting `value`. Argument validation still throws synchronously, and the fulfillment promise does not reject."},"292":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-8","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Atomics.waitAsync proposal § overview-and-background"},"293":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-9","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"294":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-10","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Atomics.waitAsync proposal § synopsis"},"295":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-11","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"296":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-12","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2024: Atomics.waitAsync"},"297":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-13","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2024-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"298":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-14","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Atomics.waitAsync for Stage 4"},"299":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-15","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2023-05-15, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"300":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-16","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"301":{"id":"/features/atomics-wait-async-17","page_id":"/features/atomics-wait-async","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics-wait-async#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2024-07-03, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"302":{"id":"/features/atomics","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"page","content":"The Atomics object","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2017"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics"},"303":{"id":"/features/atomics-0","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#the-atomics-object","content":"The Atomics object"},"304":{"id":"/features/atomics-1","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"305":{"id":"/features/atomics-2","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"306":{"id":"/features/atomics-3","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"307":{"id":"/features/atomics-4","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#compatibility","content":"Compatibility"},"308":{"id":"/features/atomics-5","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#support","content":"Support"},"309":{"id":"/features/atomics-6","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#related","content":"Related"},"310":{"id":"/features/atomics-7","page_id":"/features/atomics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/atomics#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"311":{"id":"/features/atomics-8","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#the-atomics-object","content":"A namespace object whose functions read, write, and combine shared memory cells indivisibly, and park or wake agents on a cell."},"312":{"id":"/features/atomics-9","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#what-it-does","content":"`Atomics` is a single ordinary object, not callable and not constructable, whose functions operate indivisibly on shared memory cells and let agents wait for and dispatch events. `add`, `sub`, `and`, `or`, `xor`, and `exchange` all route through `AtomicReadModifyWrite`, which loads a value, combines it with the argument, stores the result, and returns the value it loaded. The tutorial tabulates the same set together with `load`, `store`, and `compareExchange`, and states that every read-modify-write member returns the previous cell value."},"313":{"id":"/features/atomics-10","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#what-it-does","content":"`Atomics.wait` puts the calling agent into a wait queue for a cell and sleeps until it is woken or the timeout elapses, returning `\"not-equal\"`, `\"timed-out\"`, or `\"ok\"`, and `Atomics.wake` releases up to a requested count of agents queued on that cell. `Atomics.isLockFree` reports, per datum size, whether an atomic step needs a lock outside the datum, and always returns `true` for size 4. A separate memory model clause constrains the orderings these events may take, states that a race-free program behaves as an interleaving of its agents, and states that no ordering is left undefined."},"314":{"id":"/features/atomics-11","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#what-it-does","content":"The tutorial names the case that motivates the read-modify-write members: two agents incrementing one counter with a plain `++` can each load, add, and store around the other, losing an update. It names the case for the queue members as replacing a spin loop over an atomic load with sleeping until the writer signals the cell."},"315":{"id":"/features/atomics-12","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#gotchas","content":"`Atomics.wait` reports why it stopped instead of throwing, and one of its outcomes needs no other agent at all: a value argument that does not match the cell returns `\"not-equal\"` immediately, and a finite timeout that elapses returns `\"timed-out\"`. Only `Int32Array` is accepted by `wait` and `wake`, because both validate with `onlyInt32` set, and `wait` throws a `TypeError` when the surrounding agent cannot block. The tutorial states that blocking is permitted on a browser's main thread but that hosts are expected to deny it there."},"316":{"id":"/features/atomics-13","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#gotchas","content":"Every member returns a number that is easy to misread as the new state: `AtomicReadModifyWrite` returns the loaded value, so `Atomics.add(cells, 0, 3)` on a cell holding `5` evaluates to `5` while the cell becomes `8`, and `Atomics.compareExchange` returns the previous value whether or not the comparison matched."},"317":{"id":"/features/atomics-14","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#compatibility","content":"The waking function was renamed after this edition. ECMAScript 2017 specifies it as `Atomics.wake`, while ECMAScript 2019 specifies the same operation as `Atomics.notify`, which is the name the example above calls."},"318":{"id":"/features/atomics-15","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#compatibility","content":"The accepted buffer kind also widened. In this edition every member validates its argument with `ValidateSharedIntegerTypedArray`, which throws a `TypeError` unless the view's buffer is a `SharedArrayBuffer`, so `Atomics.load` over a plain `ArrayBuffer` was rejected here even though later editions accept it."},"319":{"id":"/features/atomics-16","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#support","content":"V8 shipped the operations in 6.0, whose entry states that the release gives JavaScript access to shared memory, atomics, and futexes."},"320":{"id":"/features/atomics-17","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#related","content":"shared-array-buffer"},"321":{"id":"/features/atomics-18","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#related","content":"atomics-wait-async"},"322":{"id":"/features/atomics-19","page_id":"/features/atomics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/atomics#related","content":"atomics-p