re, read, write, and round numbers using IEEE 754 binary16 values."},"773":{"id":"/features/float16-6","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#what-it-does","content":"`Float16Array` adds a two-byte floating-point typed array. `DataView.prototype.getFloat16` and `setFloat16` access binary16 fields with explicit byte order, while `Math.f16round` returns the closest representable binary16 value as a Number."},"774":{"id":"/features/float16-7","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#gotchas","content":"JavaScript arithmetic still uses Number values. Storing into `Float16Array`, writing through `setFloat16`, or calling `Math.f16round` performs the binary16 rounding step. The proposal frames the APIs as conversions at storage and rounding boundaries."},"775":{"id":"/features/float16-8","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"Float16Array proposal § proposal"},"776":{"id":"/features/float16-9","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"777":{"id":"/features/float16-10","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"Float16Array proposal § motivation"},"778":{"id":"/features/float16-11","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"779":{"id":"/features/float16-12","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2025: TypedArray objects"},"780":{"id":"/features/float16-13","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2025-06-25, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"781":{"id":"/features/float16-14","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Float16Array for Stage 4"},"782":{"id":"/features/float16-15","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2025-02-18, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"783":{"id":"/features/float16-16","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"784":{"id":"/features/float16-17","page_id":"/features/float16","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/float16#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2025-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"785":{"id":"/features/for-in-order","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"page","content":"for-in enumeration order","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2020"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order"},"786":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-0","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order#for-in-enumeration-order","content":"for-in enumeration order"},"787":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-1","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"788":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-2","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"789":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-3","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"790":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-4","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"791":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-5","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#for-in-enumeration-order","content":"`EnumerateObjectProperties` must now match a specified For-In Iterator for ordinary objects, so `for`-`in` order stops being implementation-defined in the common case."},"792":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-6","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#what-it-does","content":"`EnumerateObjectProperties` gains a requirement: unless the object or something in its prototype chain is a Proxy, an integer-indexed, a module namespace, or a host-provided exotic object, its iterator must behave as `CreateForInIterator(O)` would, and the requirement lapses once a `[[Prototype]]` changes, a property is removed, a property is added to a prototype, or an `[[Enumerable]]` attribute changes. The named iterator collects the string keys of `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, yields those whose descriptor is enumerable while recording every key it inspected, then repeats on the prototype and skips keys already recorded. Own keys therefore arrive in array-index order first, then string keys in property creation order. The same clause carries an informative generator function that reproduces the required behavior. The proposal names the other APIs that expose the same own-property order: `Object.keys`, `Object.values`, `Object.entries`, `JSON.parse` through `InternalizeJSONProperty`, and `JSON.stringify` through `SerializeJSONObject`. Those go through `EnumerableOwnPropertyNames`, which walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]` in list order and keeps only enumerable string keys, so they share the own-property sequence without walking the prototype chain."},"793":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-7","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#gotchas","content":"A non-enumerable own property removes an inherited enumerable property of the same name from the enumeration entirely, because the iterator records each inspected key before testing its `[[Enumerable]]` attribute, so the hidden `hidden` above keeps the inherited `9` out of the loop as well. Any one of the listed mutations during the loop voids the requirement rather than producing a defined fallback: deleting a property, assigning to a prototype, or calling `Object.defineProperty` to flip enumerability puts the rest of that enumeration back under the older prose. A `Proxy` anywhere in the chain is outside the guarantee even when its `ownKeys` trap returns a sorted list. Objects that `JSON.parse` builds are fully inside the interop semantics, which the proposal singles out as the case that becomes completely specified; a typed array is not, so index enumeration over one is still unconstrained by this requirement."},"794":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-8","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Specifying for-in enumeration order § for-in-ordered-apis"},"795":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-9","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"796":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-10","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: EnumerateObjectProperties ( O )"},"797":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-11","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"798":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-12","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: For-In Iterator Objects"},"799":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-13","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"800":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-14","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: OrdinaryOwnPropertyKeys ( O )"},"801":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-15","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"802":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-16","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: EnumerableOwnPropertyNames ( O, kind )"},"803":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-17","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"804":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-18","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"TC39 notes: For-in order for stage 4"},"805":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-19","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-12-04, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"806":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-20","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 119th General Assembly"},"807":{"id":"/features/for-in-order-21","page_id":"/features/for-in-order","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/for-in-order#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"808":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"page","content":"Function.prototype.toString revision","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2019"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision"},"809":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-0","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#functionprototypetostring-revision","content":"Function.prototype.toString revision"},"810":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-1","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"811":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-2","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"812":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-3","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"813":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-4","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#support","content":"Support"},"814":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-5","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"815":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-6","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#functionprototypetostring-revision","content":"`Function.prototype.toString` returns the exact source text a function was defined from, or `NativeFunction` syntax when no such text exists."},"816":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-7","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#what-it-does","content":"`Function.prototype.toString` returns `func.[[SourceText]]` whenever the receiver has that slot holding a String and `HostHasSourceTextAvailable(func)` is true; a bound function exotic object or a built-in function object instead yields an implementation-defined string with the syntax of `NativeFunction`, and for a well-known intrinsic that is not an anonymous function the `PropertyName` part of that string must be the initial value of its `name` property. Each function definition form sets `[[SourceText]]` to the source text matched by its own production, so the slot holds the characters as written rather than a normalised rendering. `CreateDynamicFunction` has no matched source text, so it synthesises one: the kind's prefix, `\" anonymous(\"`, the joined parameter string, a LINE FEED, `\") {\"`, a LINE FEED, the body text, a LINE FEED, and `\"}\"`."},"817":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-8","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#gotchas","content":"The comment and the space before the parameter list survive, because the slot stores the matched characters, so any code that compares `fn.toString()` against a canonical spelling now sees the original formatting. A bound function keeps no source text: `tally.bind(null).toString()` returns `NativeFunction` syntax whose `PropertyName` is absent even though `tally.bind(null).name` is `\"bound tally\"`, since the bound-function branch is checked before the `[[SourceText]]` branch. A function built by the `Function` constructor reports the synthesised shape rather than the arguments as passed, so its text always contains three added line feeds. A host may return `false` from `HostHasSourceTextAvailable`, which drops that function to the `NativeFunction` result as well, and the operation must answer the same way every time it is called with the same function."},"818":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-9","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#support","content":"V8 returned exact slices of source text from 6.6, whose comparison shows a comment and a space that its earlier output dropped."},"819":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-10","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Function.prototype.toString revision"},"820":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-11","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"821":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-12","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Function.prototype.toString ( )"},"822":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-13","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"823":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-14","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Function Definitions, Runtime Semantics: Evaluation"},"824":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-15","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"825":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-16","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Runtime Semantics: CreateDynamicFunction"},"826":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-17","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"827":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-18","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: HostHasSourceTextAvailable ( func )"},"828":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-19","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"829":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-20","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Function.prototype.toString revision for Stage 4"},"830":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-21","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-11-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"831":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-22","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"V8 release v6.6: Function.prototype.toString revision"},"832":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-23","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2018-03-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"833":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-24","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 117th General Assembly"},"834":{"id":"/features/function-to-string-revision-25","page_id":"/features/function-to-string-revision","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/function-to-string-revision#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"835":{"id":"/features/global-this","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"page","content":"globalThis","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2020"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this"},"836":{"id":"/features/global-this-0","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this#globalthis","content":"globalThis"},"837":{"id":"/features/global-this-1","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"838":{"id":"/features/global-this-2","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"839":{"id":"/features/global-this-3","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"840":{"id":"/features/global-this-4","page_id":"/features/global-this","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"841":{"id":"/features/global-this-5","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#globalthis","content":"`globalThis` names the global `this` value through one writable, configurable, non-enumerable property of the global object."},"842":{"id":"/features/global-this-6","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#what-it-does","content":"The property's initial value is the `[[GlobalThisValue]]` of the realm's global environment record, and its attributes are writable, non-enumerable, and configurable. Setting up a realm passes a separate `thisValue` alongside the global object and stores it in that slot, defaulting to the global object when the host supplies nothing. One spelling therefore reaches the same value in a browser, a server runtime, and an engine shell. The proposal's own case against the workarounds is concrete: the list of identifiers to probe grows with every environment, and `Function('return this')()` is refused under some content security policies. The name was chosen after attempts to ship the property as `global` broke existing sites, and it deliberately says \"global `this` value\" rather than \"global object\"."},"843":{"id":"/features/global-this-7","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#gotchas","content":"The binding is ordinary data, not a keyword: `globalThis.globalThis = 42` succeeds and every later read gives `42`, which follows from the writable and configurable attributes. Because the property is non-enumerable, `Object.keys(globalThis)` omits it, so a feature test has to use `typeof globalThis` or `\"globalThis\" in globalThis`. On the web the value is not the object that holds global variables: the proposal records that `globalThis` is the `WindowProxy`, which keeps its identity across navigation while the underlying `Window` is replaced, so a global variable can disappear from it. A top-level `this` is not a substitute either, since it is `undefined` in a module and in strict-mode function calls."},"844":{"id":"/features/global-this-8","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: globalThis § rationale"},"845":{"id":"/features/global-this-9","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"846":{"id":"/features/global-this-10","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: globalThis § naming"},"847":{"id":"/features/global-this-11","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"848":{"id":"/features/global-this-12","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: globalThis § html-and-the-windowproxy"},"849":{"id":"/features/global-this-13","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"850":{"id":"/features/global-this-14","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: globalThis"},"851":{"id":"/features/global-this-15","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"852":{"id":"/features/global-this-16","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: SetRealmGlobalObject ( realmRec, globalObj, thisValue )"},"853":{"id":"/features/global-this-17","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"854":{"id":"/features/global-this-18","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"TC39 notes: globalThis to stage 4"},"855":{"id":"/features/global-this-19","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-10-01, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"856":{"id":"/features/global-this-20","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 119th General Assembly"},"857":{"id":"/features/global-this-21","page_id":"/features/global-this","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/global-this#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"858":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"page","content":"Hashbang comments","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2023"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar"},"859":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-0","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#hashbang-comments","content":"Hashbang comments"},"860":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-1","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"861":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-2","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"862":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-3","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"863":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-4","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"864":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-5","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#hashbang-comments","content":"A leading `#!` line is part of the language grammar, so engines discard it instead of relying on the host to remove it first."},"865":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-6","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#what-it-does","content":"The `HashbangComment :: #! SingleLineCommentChars?` production makes the line a comment that is discarded from the input elements handed to the syntactic grammar. It is recognized only through the `InputElementHashbangOrRegExp` goal symbol, and that goal is used at the start of a Script or Module; `#!` stays outside the grammar elsewhere so it cannot collide with an infix use of those punctuators, and `//` remains the way to comment a later line. Command-line hosts previously stripped the line themselves before handing valid source text to the engine."},"866":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-7","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#gotchas","content":"`eval` parses with the `Script` goal and accepts a hashbang, while the `Function` constructor builds a `FunctionBody` and rejects one, so `new Function(\"#!/usr/bin/env node\\nreturn 1\")` throws a `SyntaxError`. A byte-order mark placed ahead of `#!` also stops the line from being read as a hashbang, though browsers remove that mark while fetching an external script or module, before the text reaches the parser."},"867":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-8","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Hashbang Grammar proposal"},"868":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-9","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"869":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-10","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Hashbang Grammar proposal § why-only-at-the-start-of-a-source-text"},"870":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-11","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"871":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-12","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Hashbang Grammar proposal § does-the-hashbang-syntax-work-within-eval--function"},"872":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-13","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"873":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-14","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Hashbang Grammar proposal § interaction-with-byte-order-mark"},"874":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-15","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"875":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-16","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2023: Hashbang Comments"},"876":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-17","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2023-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"877":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-18","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2023: ECMAScript Language: Lexical Grammar"},"878":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-19","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2023-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"879":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-20","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Shebang Grammar for Stage 4"},"880":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-21","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2022-07-20, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"881":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-22","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/hashbang-grammar#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 125th General Assembly"},"882":{"id":"/features/hashbang-grammar-23","page_id":"/features/hashbang-grammar","tags