lished 2025-06-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1079":{"id":"/features/json-modules-16","page_id":"/features/json-modules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-modules#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Import Attributes and JSON Modules for Stage 4"},"1080":{"id":"/features/json-modules-17","page_id":"/features/json-modules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-modules#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2024-10-08, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1081":{"id":"/features/json-modules-18","page_id":"/features/json-modules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-modules#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1082":{"id":"/features/json-modules-19","page_id":"/features/json-modules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-modules#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2025-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1083":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"page","content":"JSON source text access and raw JSON","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2026"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json"},"1084":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-0","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#json-source-text-access-and-raw-json","content":"JSON source text access and raw JSON"},"1085":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-1","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1086":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-2","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1087":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-3","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1088":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-4","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1089":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-5","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#json-source-text-access-and-raw-json","content":"JSON parsing can expose primitive source text, and stringification can emit validated raw JSON primitives."},"1090":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-6","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#what-it-does","content":"A `JSON.parse` reviver receives a third context argument whose `source` property contains the original token text for an unmodified primitive. `JSON.rawJSON` creates a frozen placeholder for validated primitive JSON text, `JSON.isRawJSON` detects it, and `JSON.stringify` emits its text without quoting it."},"1091":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-7","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#gotchas","content":"Source text is supplied only for primitive values whose source still corresponds to the visited value. `JSON.rawJSON` accepts one complete JSON primitive, not an object or array, and rejects invalid text."},"1092":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-8","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"JSON.parse source text access § proposed-solution"},"1093":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-9","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1094":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-10","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"JSON.parse source text access § discussion"},"1095":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-11","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1096":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-12","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2026: JSON object"},"1097":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-13","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2026-06-30, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1098":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-14","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"TC39 notes: JSON source text interaction"},"1099":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-15","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2025-11-18, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1100":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-16","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1101":{"id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json-17","page_id":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-source-and-raw-json#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2026-07-01, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1102":{"id":"/features/json-superset","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"page","content":"JSON superset","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2019"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset"},"1103":{"id":"/features/json-superset-0","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#json-superset","content":"JSON superset"},"1104":{"id":"/features/json-superset-1","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1105":{"id":"/features/json-superset-2","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1106":{"id":"/features/json-superset-3","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1107":{"id":"/features/json-superset-4","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#support","content":"Support"},"1108":{"id":"/features/json-superset-5","page_id":"/features/json-superset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1109":{"id":"/features/json-superset-6","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#json-superset","content":"String literals accept unescaped U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR and U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, so every JSON string is also an ECMAScript string literal."},"1110":{"id":"/features/json-superset-7","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#what-it-does","content":"`DoubleStringCharacter` and `SingleStringCharacter` each gain `<LS>` and `<PS>` alternatives beside `SourceCharacter but not one of \" or \\ or LineTerminator`, and the clause's note now lists only the closing quote, U+005C, U+000D, and U+000A as code points that cannot appear literally. The proposal states the mismatch this removes: JSON strings may carry those two code points unescaped while ECMAScript string literals could not, so `JSON.parse` claimed a subset relationship the grammar did not support. It also records that the restriction stays in regular expression literals, because those are not part of JSON, and that template literals already accepted both code points."},"1111":{"id":"/features/json-superset-8","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#gotchas","content":"Only string literals changed. Regular expression literals still exclude `LineTerminator` from `RegularExpressionNonTerminator`, so `eval(\"/a\\u2028b/\")` with a literal separator throws a `SyntaxError`. Outside string literals both code points remain line terminators, so they still close a single-line comment: `eval(\"1 // c\\u2028+ 2\")` evaluates to `3` while `eval(\"1 // c + 2\")` evaluates to `1`. The proposal also notes that accepting all JSON syntax does not make every JSON text semantically valid, so a duplicate `__proto__` key in an object literal still triggers an early error under Annex B."},"1112":{"id":"/features/json-superset-9","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#support","content":"V8 stopped treating the two separators as line terminators inside string literals in 6.6, which reports that using them previously produced a `SyntaxError`."},"1113":{"id":"/features/json-superset-10","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Subsume JSON § motivation"},"1114":{"id":"/features/json-superset-11","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1115":{"id":"/features/json-superset-12","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Subsume JSON § regular-expression-literals"},"1116":{"id":"/features/json-superset-13","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1117":{"id":"/features/json-superset-14","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Subsume JSON § validity"},"1118":{"id":"/features/json-superset-15","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1119":{"id":"/features/json-superset-16","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: String Literals"},"1120":{"id":"/features/json-superset-17","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1121":{"id":"/features/json-superset-18","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Regular Expression Literals"},"1122":{"id":"/features/json-superset-19","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1123":{"id":"/features/json-superset-20","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"TC39 notes: ECMAScript as a superset of JSON"},"1124":{"id":"/features/json-superset-21","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-05-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1125":{"id":"/features/json-superset-22","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"V8 release v6.6: JSON ⊂ ECMAScript"},"1126":{"id":"/features/json-superset-23","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2018-03-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1127":{"id":"/features/json-superset-24","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 117th General Assembly"},"1128":{"id":"/features/json-superset-25","page_id":"/features/json-superset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/json-superset#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1129":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"page","content":"Legacy RegExp features","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ESNext"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features"},"1130":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-0","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#legacy-regexp-features","content":"Legacy RegExp features"},"1131":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-1","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1132":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-2","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1133":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-3","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1134":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-4","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1135":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-5","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#legacy-regexp-features","content":"The proposal specifies legacy constructor properties and `RegExp.prototype.compile` for web compatibility."},"1136":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-6","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#what-it-does","content":"A successful match updates constructor properties such as `RegExp.$1`, `RegExp.lastMatch`, and their aliases. The accessors are configurable, so a secured environment can delete them. The proposal also limits the legacy behavior to direct, same-realm `RegExp` instances rather than proper subclasses or cross-realm objects."},"1137":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-7","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#gotchas","content":"The constructor properties are shared mutable state and can be overwritten by an unrelated successful match. The proposal places the entire feature in Annex B and does not make it a portable substitute for reading a match result."},"1138":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-8","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"TC39 active proposals at the cutoff"},"1139":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-9","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1140":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-10","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"Legacy RegExp features in JavaScript § introduction"},"1141":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-11","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1142":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-12","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"Legacy RegExp features in JavaScript § regexp"},"1143":{"id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features-13","page_id":"/features/legacy-regexp-features","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/legacy-regexp-features#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1144":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"page","content":"Logical assignment operators","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2021"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment"},"1145":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-0","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#logical-assignment-operators","content":"Logical assignment operators"},"1146":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-1","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1147":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-2","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1148":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-3","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1149":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-4","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#support","content":"Support"},"1150":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-5","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#related","content":"Related"},"1151":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-6","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/logical-assignment#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1152":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-7","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#logical-assignment-operators","content":"`&&=`, `||=`, and `??=` write to their target only when the left operand passes the test their logical operator applies."},"1153":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-8","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#what-it-does","content":"Each operator is its own production of `AssignmentExpression`, alongside the table-driven `AssignmentOperator` forms such as `+=`. The evaluation of a compound assignment always applies its binary operator and always performs `PutValue`, while `&&=` returns the left value untouched when it is falsy, `||=` returns it when it is truthy, and `??=` returns it when it is neither `undefined` nor `null`. All three reach `PutValue` only on the branch that assigns. The same clause routes an anonymous function on the right through `NamedEvaluation` when the target is an identifier reference, so the function receives the target's name; at the Stage 4 presentation the champion confirmed that this applies to all three operators and not to `+=` and its siblings, which coerce their operands to numbers. The proposal's stated aim is exactly that skipped write: a property whose setter has side effects should not be assigned a value it already holds."},"1154":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-9","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#gotchas","content":"The three operators disagree about falsy values that are not nullish: `counters.hits ||= 10` overwrites a stored `0`, while `counters.hits ??= 10` leaves it, because only `??=` tests for `undefined` and `null`. A skipped write also skips its errors: on a frozen object `config.retries ||= 5` is silent because the left value is truthy, while `config.label ||= \"default\"` on a falsy frozen property throws a `TypeError`, which follows the same clause's note that the error comes from the assignment step. Adding an anonymous function through `handler ||= function () {}` gives it the name `handler`, so a stack trace or `fn.name` check changes when the equivalent `handler = handler || function () {}` is rewritten. The saved write is observable through anything that watches the property, including a `Proxy` `set` trap, not only through an accessor."},"1155":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-10","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#support","content":"V8 shipped the three operators in 8.5, which contrasts their conditional assignment with the unconditional assignment of the mathematical and bitwise forms."},"1156":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-11","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#related","content":"nullish-coalescing"},"1157":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-12","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Logical assignment operators § semantics"},"1158":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-13","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1159":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-14","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: Assignment Operators"},"1160":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-15","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1161":{"id":"/features/logical-assignment-16","page_id":"/features/logical-assignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/logical-assignment#source