ng#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1214":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-3","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#migration","content":"Migration"},"1215":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-4","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#support","content":"Support"},"1216":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-5","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#related","content":"Related"},"1217":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-6","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1218":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-7","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#nodejs-typescript-type-stripping","content":"Node executes TypeScript files containing erasable syntax by replacing types with whitespace and running the remaining JavaScript."},"1219":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-8","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#what-it-does","content":"The built-in path performs no type checking and ignores `tsconfig.json`. It chooses CommonJS or ECMAScript-module behavior using the same package and extension rules as JavaScript, with `.mts` and `.cts` selecting fixed module systems. Imports retain explicit file extensions because Node does not rewrite specifiers."},"1220":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-9","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#gotchas","content":"Enums, parameter properties, runtime namespaces, and import aliases require JavaScript generation and error in the current stripping path. `.tsx`, TypeScript under `node_modules`, `paths` aliases, and value imports used only as types are unsupported."},"1221":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-10","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#gotchas","content":"Node.js 22.7.0 introduced `--experimental-transform-types` for syntax such as enums and namespaces, while the current documentation records that Node.js 26 removed the flag."},"1222":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-11","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#migration","content":"Use `erasableSyntaxOnly` and `verbatimModuleSyntax` during type checking, and write type-only imports with the `type` keyword. A project needing transforms, configuration-driven paths, or full syntax requires a separate TypeScript execution path."},"1223":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-12","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#support","content":"Engine or tool"},"1224":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-13","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#support","content":"Version"},"1225":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-14","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#support","content":"Source"},"1226":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping-15","page_id":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/node-typescript-type-stripping#support","content":"Retrieved"},"1227":{"id":"/features/node-typescript-type-st