atures/nonextensible-private-fields#gotchas","content":"This is an intentional behavior change for code that stamps private elements onto objects after they become non-extensible. The proposal records web-compatibility measurement because the earlier behavior has shipped."},"1268":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-8","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"TC39 active proposals at the cutoff"},"1269":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-9","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1270":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-10","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"Disallow Adding New Private Fields to Non-extensible Objects § proposal"},"1271":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-11","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1272":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-12","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"Disallow Adding New Private Fields to Non-extensible Objects § background"},"1273":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-13","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1274":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-14","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"Disallow Adding New Private Fields to Non-extensible Objects § web-compatibility"},"1275":{"id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields-15","page_id":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nonextensible-private-fields#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1276":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"page","content":"Nullish coalescing operator","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2020"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing"},"1277":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-0","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#nullish-coalescing-operator","content":"Nullish coalescing operator"},"1278":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-1","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1279":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-2","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1280":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-3","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1281":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-4","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#support","content":"Support"},"1282":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-5","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#related","content":"Related"},"1283":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-6","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1284":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-7","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#nullish-coalescing-operator","content":"`??` evaluates its right operand only when the left one is `null` or `undefined`, leaving other falsy values in place."},"1285":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-8","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#what-it-does","content":"`CoalesceExpression` takes a `CoalesceExpressionHead` on the left and a `BitwiseORExpression` on the right, and it sits beside `LogicalORExpression` under `ShortCircuitExpression`; the head may itself be a `CoalesceExpression` or a `BitwiseORExpression`, and nothing more. That shape means no production accepts an unparenthesized `&&` or `||` next to `??`. Evaluation returns the left value unless it is `undefined` or `null`, in which case the right operand is evaluated and returned; `&&` and `||` instead branch on `ToBoolean` of the left value. The proposal's own list of the cases `||` gets wrong is a header string of `\"\"`, an animation duration of `0`, and a `false` flag, each of which `||` replaces with the default."},"1286":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-9","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#gotchas","content":"Mixing the operators without parentheses is a `SyntaxError` rather than a precedence puzzle: `null || 1 ?? 2` and `null ?? 1 || 2` both fail to parse, while `(null || 1) ?? 2` evaluates to `1`. The rejection is a grammatical one, so it is reported before any code runs, even in a branch that would never execute. Replacing `||` with `??` in existing code changes behavior wherever a value can be `\"\"`, `0`, `NaN`, or `false`, and leaves it unchanged only for `null` and `undefined`. The operator does not test a property's presence, so a missing property and a property explicitly set to `undefined` both take the right operand."},"1287":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-10","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#support","content":"V8 shipped the operator in 8.0, whose notes give `props.enabled || true` as the defaulting bug it addresses."},"1288":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-11","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#related","content":"optional-chaining"},"1289":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-12","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#related","content":"logical-assignment"},"1290":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-13","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Nullish Coalescing for JavaScript § overview-and-motivation"},"1291":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-14","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1292":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-15","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: Binary Logical Operators"},"1293":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-16","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1294":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-17","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: Binary Logical Operators, Runtime Semantics: Evaluation"},"1295":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-18","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1296":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-19","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Nullish Coalescing for Stage 4"},"1297":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-20","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-12-04, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1298":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-21","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"V8 release v8.0: Nullish coalescing"},"1299":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-22","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"Engine record, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1300":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-23","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 119th General Assembly"},"1301":{"id":"/features/nullish-coalescing-24","page_id":"/features/nullish-coalescing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/nullish-coalescing#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1302":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"page","content":"Numeric separators","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2021"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators"},"1303":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-0","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#numeric-separators","content":"Numeric separators"},"1304":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-1","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1305":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-2","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1306":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-3","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1307":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-4","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#support","content":"Support"},"1308":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-5","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1309":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-6","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#numeric-separators","content":"A single `_` between two digits of a numeric literal groups them for reading and leaves the literal's value unchanged."},"1310":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-7","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#what-it-does","content":"`NumericLiteralSeparator :: _` is threaded through the digit productions by a `[Sep]` parameter, so decimal, binary, octal, hexadecimal, fractional, exponent, and BigInt digits each accept it. Every one of those productions places the separator between two digits, which is what rules out a leading, trailing, or doubled separator, and the `0` form of `DecimalIntegerLiteral` has no continuation, so a separator cannot follow a lone leading zero. Annex B's legacy octal and legacy-octal-like productions take no `[Sep]` parameter, so the old `0`-prefixed forms stay separator-free. The proposal's stated aim is reading long digit runs, including fixed-point amounts whose scale is otherwise invisible. It also records the deliberately restrictive rule: consecutive separators and separators next to the ends of a literal were left out because no practical use was found."},"1311":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-8","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#gotchas","content":"The separator belongs to source text, not to numeric parsing, so `StringNumericLiteral` uses the digit productions with separators disabled and `Number(\"1_000\")` is `NaN`, while `parseInt` reads the longest prefix its grammar accepts and `parseInt(\"1_000\", 10)` returns `1`: user input carrying separators has to be stripped before conversion. A BigInt cannot separate its suffix, so `1_000n` is valid and `1_n` is a `SyntaxError`, which the proposal spells out for the `n` suffix. Digits inside a Unicode code point escape are not numeric literals, so `\"\\u{1_F600}\"` is a `SyntaxError`; the Stage 4 discussion settled that question against allowing underscores there."},"1312":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-9","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#support","content":"V8 shipped separators in 7.5, whose example regroups a thirteen-digit literal per thousand to make its magnitude readable."},"1313":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-10","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Numeric separators § motivation"},"1314":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-11","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1315":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-12","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Numeric separators § alternative-syntax"},"1316":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-13","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1317":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-14","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Numeric separators § bigint-literal"},"1318":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-15","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1319":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-16","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: Numeric Literals"},"1320":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-17","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1321":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-18","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: Annex B Numeric Literals"},"1322":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-19","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1323":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-20","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: ToNumber Applied to the String Type"},"1324":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-21","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1325":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-22","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: parseInt ( string, radix )"},"1326":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-23","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1327":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-24","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"TC39 notes: NumericLiteralSeparator for Stage 4"},"1328":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-25","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2020-07-21, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1329":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-26","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"V8 release v7.5: Numeric separators in JavaScript"},"1330":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-27","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"Engine record, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1331":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-28","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 121st General Assembly"},"1332":{"id":"/features/numeric-separators-29","page_id":"/features/numeric-separators","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/numeric-separators#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1333":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"page","content":"Object.fromEntries","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2019"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries"},"1334":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-0","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#objectfromentries","content":"Object.fromEntries"},"1335":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-1","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1336":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-2","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1337":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-3","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1338":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-4","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#support","content":"Support"},"1339":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-5","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1340":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-6","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#objectfromentries","content":"`Object.fromEntries` builds a plain object from an iterable of key-value pairs, inverting `Object.entries`."},"1341":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-7","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#what-it-does","content":"`Object.fromEntries` calls `RequireObjectCoercible` on its argument, creates an ordinary object with `%ObjectPrototype%`, asserts that the new object is extensible and has no own properties, and hands `AddEntriesFromIterable` an adder built from the `CreateDataPropertyOnObject` steps. That adder defines each pair with `CreateDataPropertyOrThrow`, which writes a writable, enumerable, configurable own property through `[[DefineOwnProperty]]` rather than through an assignment. The proposal names the gap it closes: an object could already be turned into a `Map` with `new Map(Object.entries(obj))`, but the reverse direction needed a helper or an inline reducer."},"1342":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-8","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#gotchas","content":"A pair whose key is `\"__proto__\"` becomes an own data property holding that value, because definition bypasses the inherited `__proto__` setter that an assignment loop would trigger; the loop above leaves `Object.prototype` in place instead. Duplicate keys keep the last pair, since each definition overwrites the previous property. The result always has `%ObjectPrototype%`, so it is never a null-prototype object even when the input came from one. Each entry is read as two property gets, `\"0\"` and `\"1\"`, after a check that the entry is an object, so a three-element entry silently loses its tail and a number in the input throws a `TypeError` while closing the iterator."},"1343":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-9","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#support","content":"V8 added the built-in in 7.3, whose example feeds it the array returned by `Object.entries` to recover an equivalent object."},"1344":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-10","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Object.fromEntries § rationale"},"1345":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-11","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1346":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-12","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Object.fromEntries ( iterable )"},"1347":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-13","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1348":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-14","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: CreateDataPropertyOrThrow ( O, P, V )"},"1349":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-15","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1350":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-16","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: AddEntriesFromIterable ( target, iterable, adder )"},"1351":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-17","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1352":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-18","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Object.fromEntries for Stage 4"},"1353":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-19","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-01-29, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1354":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-20","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"V8 release v7.3: Object.fromEntries"},"1355":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-21","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2019-02-07, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1356":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-22","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 117th General Assembly"},"1357":{"id":"/features/object-from-entries-23","page_id":"/features/object-from-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-from-entries#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1358":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"page","content":"Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2017"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors"},"1359":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-0","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#objectgetownpropertydescriptors","content":"Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors"},"1360":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-1","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1361":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-2","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1362":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-3","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1363":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-4","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1364":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-5","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#objectgetownpropertydescriptors","content":"One call returns an object mapping every own property key to that property's descriptor object."},"1365":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-6","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#what-it-does","content":"The method coerces its argument with `ToObject`, walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]` in list order, converts each descriptor with `FromPropertyDescriptor`, and defines the result as an own data property of a fresh plain object. `FromPropertyDescriptor` produces the `value`, `writable`, `get`, `set`, `enumerable`, and `configurable` fields that the descriptor actually has, and returns `undefined` for an absent descriptor. Symbol keys and non-enumerable keys are both included, and the result is shaped for direct use as the second argument of `Object.create` or `Object.defineProperties`."},"1366":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-7","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#what-it-does","content":"The proposal targets copying: `Object.assign` reads through `[[Get]]` and writes through `[[Set]]`, which turns an accessor into a plain value on the target and discards attributes, so composing prototypes and mixins whose methods are non-enumerable needed hand-written descriptor collection. The proposal's own examples are a shallow clone through `Object.create` and a merge through `Object.defineProperties`."},"1367":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-8","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#gotchas","content":"The returned map is not a faithful copy of attributes at its own level. Every entry is created with `CreateDataProperty`, so a descriptor describing a non-writable, non-configurable property is itself stored as a writable, enumerable, configurable property of the result."},"1368":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-9","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#gotchas","content":"A Proxy can drop keys from the result. A key whose descriptor comes back `undefined` is skipped rather than mapped to `undefined`, so a `getOwnPropertyDescriptor` trap returning `undefined` for `b` produces a result whose own keys are only `[\"a\"]`. Primitive arguments are coerced instead of rejected, so `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(\"ab\")` describes the index properties and `length` of a wrapper."},"1369":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-10","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors proposal: Motivation"},"1370":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-11","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1371":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-12","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors proposal: Illustrative Examples"},"1372":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-13","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1373":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-14","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors ( O )"},"1374":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-15","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1375":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-16","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: FromPropertyDescriptor ( Desc )"},"1376":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-17","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1377":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-18","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors"},"1378":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-19","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2016-05-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1379":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-20","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Last results of the 113th Ecma General Assembly held in Montreux, June 27, 2017"},"1380":{"id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors-21","page_id":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-get-own-property-descriptors#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1381":{"id":"/features/object-has-own","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"page","content":"Object.hasOwn","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2022"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own"},"1382":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-0","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#objecthasown","content":"Object.hasOwn"},"1383":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-1","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1384":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-2","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1385":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-3","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1386":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-4","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#support","content":"Support"},"1387":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-5","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1388":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-6","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#objecthasown","content":"`Object.hasOwn(target, key)` reports an own property without routing the call through the target's own prototype chain."},"1389":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-7","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#what-it-does","content":"`Object.hasOwn` coerces its first argument with `ToObject`, its second with `ToPropertyKey`, and returns the result of `HasOwnProperty`. `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty` performs the same two conversions in the opposite order, and the champion described that reordering as the whole functional difference, observable only in which conversion throws first. The two cases the proposal targets are objects that never inherit the method and objects that shadow it: `Object.create(null)` produces a target on which `hasOwnProperty` is not a function, and an own `hasOwnProperty` member can make the call do something else entirely."},"1390":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-8","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#gotchas","content":"\"Own\" is literal: an inherited property answers `false`, so `Object.hasOwn(Object.create({ a: 1 }), \"a\")` reports nothing even though `\"a\" in target` would be `true`. Because the target passes through `ToObject`, a primitive is boxed instead of rejected and `Object.hasOwn(\"ab\", 1)` is `true`, while `null` and `undefined` throw a `TypeError`. Array holes count as absent, so `Object.hasOwn([1, , 3], 1)` is `false` while index `1` still reads as `undefined`. The key is a property key, not a string comparison: `Object.hasOwn({ \"1\": true }, 1)` is `true`."},"1391":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-9","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#support","content":"`Object.hasOwn` shipped in V8 9.3, which that release describes as an easier-to-reach alias for `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call`."},"1392":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-10","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Accessible Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty proposal § objectcreatenull"},"1393":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-11","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1394":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-12","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Accessible Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty proposal § redefining-hasownproperty"},"1395":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-13","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1396":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-14","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2022: Object.hasOwn"},"1397":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-15","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2022-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1398":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-16","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2022: Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty"},"1399":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-17","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2022-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1400":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-18","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Accessible Object hasOwnProperty for Stage 4"},"1401":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-19","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2021-08-31, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1402":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-20","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"V8 release v9.3: Object.hasOwn"},"1403":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-21","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Engine record, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1404":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-22","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 123rd General Assembly"},"1405":{"id":"/features/object-has-own-23","page_id":"/features/object-has-own","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-has-own#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2022-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1406":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"page","content":"Object rest and spread properties","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2018"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread"},"1407":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-0","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#object-rest-and-spread-properties","content":"Object rest and spread properties"},"1408":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-1","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1409":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-2","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1410":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-3","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1411":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-4","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#support","content":"Support"},"1412":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-5","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1413":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-6","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#object-rest-and-spread-properties","content":"Object literals accept `...source` to copy properties in, and object patterns accept `...target` to collect the properties they did not name."},"1414":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-7","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#what-it-does","content":"The object initializer grammar gains `PropertyDefinition : ... AssignmentExpression`. Evaluating that production calls `CopyDataProperties` on the object under construction with an empty exclusion list. `CopyDataProperties` walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, skips any key whose own descriptor is not enumerable, and installs the rest with `CreateDataProperty`; a `source` of `undefined` or `null` leaves the target untouched."},"1415":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-8","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#what-it-does","content":"`ObjectBindingPattern` gains a `BindingRestProperty` alternative, both alone and after a property list. `RestDestructuringAssignmentEvaluation` creates an object with `%ObjectPrototype%` and copies into it every property except the names the pattern already bound. The proposal frames the pair as the object counterparts of array rest elements and array spread elements, and names the rest form's job as collecting the remaining own enumerable keys the pattern did not pick off."},"1416":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-9","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#gotchas","content":"Spread is not `Object.assign` with new syntax. `CopyDataProperties` installs each property with `CreateDataProperty`, while `Object.assign` writes through `Set`, so an inherited setter on the destination runs in the second case only:"},"1417":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-10","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#gotchas","content":"Both forms read own properties only, so `{ ...Object.create({ base: 1 }) }` is `{}`; the proposal calls this out as the reason a spread is not a general clone. A rest target always receives a plain object: `Object.getPrototypeOf(rest) === Object.prototype` holds even when the source had another prototype, because the evaluation step passes `%ObjectPrototype%` to `ObjectCreate`. Spreading a string spreads its index properties, so `{ ...\"hi\" }` is `{ \"0\": \"h\", \"1\": \"i\" }`."},"1418":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-11","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#support","content":"V8 shipped both halves in 6.0, whose examples destructure with a rest binding and rebuild the object with a spread."},"1419":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-12","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Object Rest/Spread Properties for ECMAScript"},"1420":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-13","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1421":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-14","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Object Rest/Spread Properties: Rest Properties"},"1422":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-15","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1423":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-16","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Object Rest/Spread Properties: Known Issues"},"1424":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-17","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1425":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-18","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Object Initializer"},"1426":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-19","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1427":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-20","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Runtime Semantics: PropertyDefinitionEvaluation"},"1428":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-21","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1429":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-22","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: CopyDataProperties"},"1430":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-23","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1431":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-24","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Destructuring Binding Patterns"},"1432":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-25","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1433":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-26","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Runtime Semantics: RestDestructuringAssignmentEvaluation"},"1434":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-27","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1435":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-28","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Object.assign"},"1436":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-29","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1437":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-30","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Rest/Spread properties for stage 4"},"1438":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-31","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-01-23, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1439":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-32","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"V8 release v6.0: Object rest/spread properties"},"1440":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-33","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2017-06-09, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1441":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-34","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Results of the 115th Ecma General Assembly held in Geneva, June 27, 2018"},"1442":{"id":"/features/object-rest-spread-35","page_id":"/features/object-rest-spread","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-rest-spread#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1443":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"page","content":"Object.values and Object.entries","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2017"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries"},"1444":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-0","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#objectvalues-and-objectentries","content":"Object.values and Object.entries"},"1445":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-1","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1446":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-2","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1447":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-3","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1448":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-4","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#related","content":"Related"},"1449":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-5","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1450":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-6","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#objectvalues-and-objectentries","content":"Two static methods return an object's own enumerable string-keyed values, and its key-value pairs, as arrays."},"1451":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-7","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#what-it-does","content":"`Object.values` coerces its argument with `ToObject` and returns `EnumerableOwnProperties(obj, \"value\")` as an array, and `Object.entries` does the same with the kind `\"key+value\"`, so each element is a two-element array. That abstract operation walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, keeps only keys whose type is String and whose descriptor is enumerable, reads each value with `Get`, and orders the result the way `EnumerateObjectProperties` would."},"1452":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-8","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#what-it-does","content":"The proposal names the target cases: obtaining own values when an object is used as a hash, and obtaining pairs for iteration, serialization, or feeding `new Map`, whose constructor already accepts an iterable of entries. It returns arrays rather than iterators to match `Object.keys`, and records that an iterator form would instead have been `Reflect.ownValues` and `Reflect.ownEntries`."},"1453":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-9","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#gotchas","content":"A getter can shrink the result while it is being built. The keys are collected first, but each value is read with `Get` during the same walk, so an accessor that deletes a later property makes `Object.entries` return fewer pairs than `Object.keys` returns keys:"},"1454":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-10","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#gotchas","content":"Symbol keys never appear, because the walk skips every key whose type is not String, and non-enumerable own properties are skipped by the same test. A primitive argument is coerced instead of rejected: `ToObject` runs first, so `Object.entries(\"hi\")` yields `[[\"0\",\"h\"],[\"1\",\"i\"]]`. Integer-like keys come first in ascending numeric order rather than in insertion order, because that is the order `EnumerateObjectProperties` produces."},"1455":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-11","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#related","content":"object-from-entries"},"1456":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-12","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#related","content":"for-in-order"},"1457":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-13","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Object.values/Object.entries: Rationale"},"1458":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-14","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1459":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-15","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Object.values/Object.entries: Iterators or Arrays?"},"1460":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-16","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1461":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-17","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: Object.values ( O )"},"1462":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-18","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1463":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-19","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: Object.entries ( O )"},"1464":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-20","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1465":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-21","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2017: EnumerableOwnProperties ( O, kind )"},"1466":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-22","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1467":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-23","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Object.values & Object.entries"},"1468":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-24","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2016-03-29, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1469":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-25","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Last results of the 113th Ecma General Assembly held in Montreux, June 27, 2017"},"1470":{"id":"/features/object-values-entries-26","page_id":"/features/object-values-entries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/object-values-entries#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2017-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1471":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"page","content":"Optional catch binding","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2019"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding"},"1472":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-0","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#optional-catch-binding","content":"Optional catch binding"},"1473":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-1","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1474":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-2","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1475":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-3","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1476":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-4","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#support","content":"Support"},"1477":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-5","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1478":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-6","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#optional-catch-binding","content":"A `catch` clause may drop both its parameter and the surrounding parentheses when the handler never reads the thrown value."},"1479":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-7","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#what-it-does","content":"The `Catch` production gains a second alternative, `catch Block`, alongside `catch ( CatchParameter ) Block`. `CatchClauseEvaluation` for that alternative returns the result of evaluating the `Block` directly, without creating a declarative environment record or running `BindingInitialization`, both of which the parameterised alternative still performs. The proposal names the patterns it targets: probing for an unimplemented web feature, setting a success flag, and parsing possibly malformed JSON with a fallback value, each of which writes a binding it never reads."},"1480":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-8","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#gotchas","content":"The earlier `catch ({})` workaround is not equivalent: destructuring a thrown `null` or `undefined` raises a `TypeError` from inside the handler, so the original exception is replaced. The proposal identifies that completion value as the one observable difference between the pattern and the new grammar, and the parameterless clause runs no `BindingInitialization`, so it cannot fail that way. The parentheses go with the parameter: `try {} catch () {}` is a `SyntaxError`, because the grammar offers only `catch ( CatchParameter ) Block` and `catch Block`."},"1481":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-9","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#support","content":"V8 accepted the clause without a parameter in 6.6, whose example handles an exception with no binding in scope."},"1482":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-10","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: optional catch binding"},"1483":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-11","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1484":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-12","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: The try Statement"},"1485":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-13","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1486":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-14","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2019: Runtime Semantics: CatchClauseEvaluation"},"1487":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-15","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1488":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-16","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Optional catch binding for stage 4"},"1489":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-17","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-05-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1490":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-18","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"V8 release v6.6: Optional catch binding"},"1491":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-19","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2018-03-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1492":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-20","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 117th General Assembly"},"1493":{"id":"/features/optional-catch-binding-21","page_id":"/features/optional-catch-binding","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-catch-binding#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2019-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1494":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"page","content":"Optional chaining","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2020"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining"},"1495":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-0","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#optional-chaining","content":"Optional chaining"},"1496":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-1","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1497":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-2","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1498":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-3","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1499":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-4","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#support","content":"Support"},"1500":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-5","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#related","content":"Related"},"1501":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-6","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1502":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-7","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#optional-chaining","content":"`?.` yields `undefined` when the value to its left is `null` or `undefined`, and skips the rest of the chain that follows it."},"1503":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-8","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#what-it-does","content":"An `OptionalExpression` evaluates its base, returns `undefined` when that value is `null` or `undefined`, and otherwise passes the base value and its reference to `ChainEvaluation` of the `OptionalChain`. `ChainEvaluation` handles the three forms — `?.IdentifierName`, `?.[Expression]`, and `?.Arguments` — and each further link of a chain recurses through the same operation, which is why one nullish check governs the whole chain. `?.` is its own punctuator with a lookahead that excludes a following decimal digit, so `flag?.3:0` remains a conditional expression. The proposal describes that reach as long short-circuiting: a triggered check skips not only the current access but the whole chain of accesses and calls that directly follows. Parentheses end a chain, so `(a?.b).c` re-enters ordinary member access on the `undefined` result."},"1504":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-9","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#gotchas","content":"The check tests only the value in front of the operator, so `config.server?.log(\"x\")` still throws a `TypeError` when `server` exists and `log` is missing; guarding the call needs a second operator, as in `config.server?.log?.(\"x\")`. Wrapping a chain in parentheses restores the failure: `(empty.server?.port).toFixed(0)` throws because grouping limits the scope of short-circuiting. `delete empty.server?.port` reports `true` without touching anything, which the proposal presents as falling out of how `delete` treats a non-reference. Three neighbouring forms are rejected by the grammar rather than made optional: `new a?.()`, a tagged template after a chain, and `a?.b = 1`; the proposal lists them as deliberately unsupported so support could be added later. The digit lookahead means the token is context-sensitive: `flag?.3:0` evaluates the conditional and prints `0.3` when `flag` is truthy."},"1505":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-10","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#support","content":"V8 shipped the operator in 8.0, whose notes frame it as replacing guards that test each intermediate value before a property access."},"1506":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-11","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#related","content":"nullish-coalescing"},"1507":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-12","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Optional Chaining for JavaScript § long-short-circuiting"},"1508":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-13","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1509":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-14","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Optional Chaining for JavaScript § edge-case-grouping"},"1510":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-15","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1511":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-16","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Optional Chaining for JavaScript § not-supported"},"1512":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-17","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1513":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-18","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: Optional Chains"},"1514":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-19","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1515":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-20","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: Optional Chains, Runtime Semantics: ChainEvaluation"},"1516":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-21","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1517":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-22","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2020: Punctuators"},"1518":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-23","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1519":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-24","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Optional Chaining for Stage 4"},"1520":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-25","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2019-12-04, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1521":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-26","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"V8 release v8.0: Optional chaining"},"1522":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-27","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"Engine record, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1523":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-28","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 119th General Assembly"},"1524":{"id":"/features/optional-chaining-29","page_id":"/features/optional-chaining","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/optional-chaining#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2020-06-16, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1525":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"page","content":"Private methods and accessors","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2022"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors"},"1526":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-0","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#private-methods-and-accessors","content":"Private methods and accessors"},"1527":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-1","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1528":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-2","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1529":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-3","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1530":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-4","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#related","content":"Related"},"1531":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-5","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1532":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-6","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#private-methods-and-accessors","content":"A method, getter, or setter named with a leading `#` is installed on each instance and is unreachable through property access."},"1533":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-7","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#what-it-does","content":"A `MethodDefinition` names its member with a `ClassElementName`, which may be a `PrivateIdentifier`. When that name is a Private Name, method evaluation returns a PrivateElement whose `[[Kind]]` is `method` for a plain definition and `accessor` for a `get` or `set` definition, instead of defining a property. Those PrivateElements are copied onto every new instance before its field initializers run. Adding a private method to an object that already carries the same private name throws a `TypeError`. The three class-features proposals were requested in one agenda item, and the sub-proposal's own repository now records only that it merged into class fields."},"1534":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-8","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#gotchas","content":"A private method is not a prototype method: instance initialization appends it to the object itself, so `Reflect.ownKeys(C.prototype)` still reports only `constructor` for a class whose sole member is `#m()`, and every instance carries its own entry. Calling one through `Object.create(C.prototype)` throws a `TypeError`, because that object never ran instance initialization. Assignment distinguishes the three kinds: `PrivateSet` throws for a `method` entry and for an `accessor` entry whose `[[Set]]` is undefined, so `this.#m = 1` and a write to a getter-only `#v` both fail where a private field would have accepted the value."},"1535":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-9","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#related","content":"class-fields"},"1536":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-10","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#related","content":"static-class-features"},"1537":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-11","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#related","content":"ergonomic-brand-checks"},"1538":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-12","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#sources","content":"Private instance methods and accessors proposal, merged into class fields § this-proposal-has-been-merged-into-the-class-fields-proposal"},"1539":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-13","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1540":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors-14","page_id":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/private-methods-and-accessors#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2022: Class Definitions grammar"},"1541":{"id":"/features/private-methods-and-ac