r","content":"Before / after"},"1604":{"id":"/features/promise-any-3","page_id":"/features/promise-any","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-any#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1605":{"id":"/features/promise-any-4","page_id":"/features/promise-any","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-any#support","content":"Support"},"1606":{"id":"/features/promise-any-5","page_id":"/features/promise-any","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-any#related","content":"Related"},"1607":{"id":"/features/promise-any-6","page_id":"/features/promise-any","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1608":{"id":"/features/promise-any-7","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#promiseany","content":"`Promise.any` settles as soon as one input promise fulfils, and rejects with an `AggregateError` only when every input has rejected."},"1609":{"id":"/features/promise-any-8","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#what-it-does","content":"`Promise.any` resolves each iterated value through its constructor's `resolve` and returns a promise that the first fulfilment settles. `PerformPromiseAny` keeps one `errors` list plus a remaining-elements counter, appending an `undefined` placeholder per input and attaching the capability's own resolve function as `onFulfilled`. Each rejection runs a reject element function that writes the reason into its reserved index, decrements the counter, and rejects the outer promise with a new `AggregateError` when the counter reaches zero. The proposal places the combinator opposite `Promise.all`, which short-circuits on the first rejection instead of the first fulfilment."},"1610":{"id":"/features/promise-any-9","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#gotchas","content":"An empty iterable rejects instead of fulfilling: the counter starts at one and drops to zero as soon as iteration finishes, so `Promise.any([])` produces an `AggregateError` whose `errors` is empty, which is the mirror image of `Promise.all([])` fulfilling with `[]`. Reasons are stored by input index rather than by settlement order, because each reject element function carries the index assigned when its input was queued, so `Promise.any([slowFailure, fastFailure])` reports `[slow, fast]`. A failure to iterate is not aggregated: an abrupt completion while getting the iterator rejects the returned promise with that exception, so a throwing `Symbol.iterator` surfaces a `RangeError` and no `errors` array exists to inspect."},"1611":{"id":"/features/promise-any-10","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#support","content":"V8 added the combinator together with its rejection type in 8.5, which describes the returned promise resolving as soon as one input promise is fulfilled."},"1612":{"id":"/features/promise-any-11","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#related","content":"aggregate-error"},"1613":{"id":"/features/promise-any-12","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#related","content":"promise-all-settled"},"1614":{"id":"/features/promise-any-13","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: Promise.any + AggregateError § motivation"},"1615":{"id":"/features/promise-any-14","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1616":{"id":"/features/promise-any-15","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: Promise.any ( iterable )"},"1617":{"id":"/features/promise-any-16","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1618":{"id":"/features/promise-any-17","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: PerformPromiseAny"},"1619":{"id":"/features/promise-any-18","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1620":{"id":"/features/promise-any-19","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2021: Promise.any Reject Element Functions"},"1621":{"id":"/features/promise-any-20","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1622":{"id":"/features/promise-any-21","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Promise.any & AggregateError for Stage 4"},"1623":{"id":"/features/promise-any-22","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2020-07-21, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1624":{"id":"/features/promise-any-23","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"V8 release v8.5: Promise.any and AggregateError"},"1625":{"id":"/features/promise-any-24","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"Engine record, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1626":{"id":"/features/promise-any-25","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards at the 121st General Assembly"},"1627":{"id":"/features/promise-any-26","page_id":"/features/promise-any","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-any#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2021-06-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1628":{"id":"/features/promise-finally","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"page","content":"Promise.prototype.finally","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2018"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally"},"1629":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-0","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#promiseprototypefinally","content":"Promise.prototype.finally"},"1630":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-1","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1631":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-2","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1632":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-3","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1633":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-4","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#support","content":"Support"},"1634":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-5","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1635":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-6","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#promiseprototypefinally","content":"A callback registered with `.finally` runs once the promise settles and passes the original outcome through unchanged."},"1636":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-7","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#what-it-does","content":"The method reads the species constructor of the receiver, builds a `Then Finally` function and a `Catch Finally` function that both close over the callback, and calls `then` with the pair. The `Then Finally` function invokes the callback with no arguments, resolves its result, and then returns a thunk producing the original fulfillment value. The `Catch Finally` function does the same and then rethrows the original rejection reason."},"1637":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-8","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#what-it-does","content":"The proposal names cleanup as the case it serves: hiding a spinner, closing file handles, or logging completion, where the outcome itself does not matter. It lists four differences from `then(f, f)`: one callback expression instead of two, no argument passed to the callback, a fulfillment value that survives, and a rejection reason that survives."},"1638":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-9","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#gotchas","content":"A value returned from the callback is discarded, because the `Then Finally` function returns a thunk for the original value rather than the callback's result, so `Promise.resolve(2).finally(() => 99)` still fulfills with `2`. Throwing is the one way through: the proposal notes that a `throw` or a rejected promise from the callback rejects the derived promise with that reason, which is the point where the parallel with syntactic cleanup is deliberately incomplete, since there is no way to distinguish a normal completion from an early return of `undefined`."},"1639":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-10","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#gotchas","content":"The callback receives nothing at all: `Promise.prototype.finally.length` is `1` while the argument count seen inside the callback is `0`, and the callback also runs on a rejected promise without marking it handled, so a `.finally` at the end of a chain still produces an unhandled rejection. When the callback is not callable, it is passed straight through to `then` in both positions, which makes `Promise.resolve(2).finally(null)` behave like `then(null, null)`."},"1640":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-11","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#support","content":"V8 shipped the method in 6.3, which introduces it as a way to clean up after a promise settles."},"1641":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-12","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Promise.prototype.finally: Rationale"},"1642":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-13","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1643":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-14","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Promise.prototype.finally: Why not .then(f, f)?"},"1644":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-15","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1645":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-16","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Promise.prototype.finally: Naming"},"1646":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-17","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1647":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-18","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Promise.prototype.finally"},"1648":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-19","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1649":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-20","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Then Finally Functions"},"1650":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-21","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1651":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-22","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2018: Catch Finally Functions"},"1652":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-23","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1653":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-24","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Promise.prototype.finally for stage 4"},"1654":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-25","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2018-01-24, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1655":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-26","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"V8 release v6.3: JavaScript language features"},"1656":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-27","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2017-10-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1657":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-28","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Results of the 115th Ecma General Assembly held in Geneva, June 27, 2018"},"1658":{"id":"/features/promise-finally-29","page_id":"/features/promise-finally","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-finally#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1659":{"id":"/features/promise-try","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"page","content":"Promise.try","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2025"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try"},"1660":{"id":"/features/promise-try-0","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try#promisetry","content":"Promise.try"},"1661":{"id":"/features/promise-try-1","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1662":{"id":"/features/promise-try-2","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1663":{"id":"/features/promise-try-3","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1664":{"id":"/features/promise-try-4","page_id":"/features/promise-try","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1665":{"id":"/features/promise-try-5","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#promisetry","content":"`Promise.try` invokes a callback and represents its return value or thrown exception with a promise."},"1666":{"id":"/features/promise-try-6","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#what-it-does","content":"The callback runs synchronously. Its returned value is used to resolve the new promise, and a synchronous exception rejects that promise. Additional arguments after the callback are passed to the callback without creating a wrapper function."},"1667":{"id":"/features/promise-try-7","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#gotchas","content":"`Promise.resolve(callback())` does not capture an exception thrown before `Promise.resolve` is called. `Promise.try` performs the call inside its promise-producing operation."},"1668":{"id":"/features/promise-try-8","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"Promise.try proposal § rationale"},"1669":{"id":"/features/promise-try-9","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1670":{"id":"/features/promise-try-10","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2025: Promise.try"},"1671":{"id":"/features/promise-try-11","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2025-06-25, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1672":{"id":"/features/promise-try-12","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Promise.try for Stage 4"},"1673":{"id":"/features/promise-try-13","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2024-10-09, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1674":{"id":"/features/promise-try-14","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1675":{"id":"/features/promise-try-15","page_id":"/features/promise-try","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-try#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2025-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1676":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"page","content":"Promise.withResolvers","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2024"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers"},"1677":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-0","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#promisewithresolvers","content":"Promise.withResolvers"},"1678":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-1","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1679":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-2","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1680":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-3","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1681":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-4","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1682":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-5","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#promisewithresolvers","content":"`Promise.withResolvers()` returns a promise together with its resolve and reject functions."},"1683":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-6","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#what-it-does","content":"The method creates the same capability supplied to a promise executor, but exposes `{ promise, resolve, reject }` without assigning outer variables from the executor callback. It invokes the constructor used as its receiver, so promise subclasses receive their own instances."},"1684":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-7","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#gotchas","content":"The exposed functions settle only their paired promise. Calling either function after settlement has no further effect, following ordinary promise resolution rules. The returned functions come from the standard promise capability algorithms."},"1685":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-8","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"Promise.withResolvers proposal § synopsis"},"1686":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-9","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1687":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-10","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2024: Promise.withResolvers"},"1688":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-11","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2024-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1689":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-12","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"TC39 notes: Promise.withResolvers for Stage 4"},"1690":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-13","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2023-11-27, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"1691":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-14","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1692":{"id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers-15","page_id":"/features/promise-with-resolvers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/promise-with-resolvers#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2024-07-03, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"1693":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"page","content":"Redeclarable global eval-introduced vars","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2025"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars"},"1694":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-0","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#redeclarable-global-eval-introduced-vars","content":"Redeclarable global eval-introduced vars"},"1695":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-1","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1696":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-2","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1697":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-3","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1698":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-4","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#compatibility","content":"Compatibility"},"1699":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-5","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1700":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-6","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#redeclarable-global-eval-introduced-vars","content":"A later global lexical declaration can shadow a configurable global property created by indirect `eval`."},"1701":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-7","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#what-it-does","content":"The change removes the global environment record's special list of names introduced by `var` inside `eval`. `HasRestrictedGlobalProperty` now reports a restriction only for a non-configurable own property of the global object, so a later script can declare the same name with `let`, `const`, or `class` and shadow the configurable property on `globalThis`. The specification returns false for a configurable existing property, which is the revised rule the proposal requested."},"1702":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-8","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#gotchas","content":"This behavior concerns re-entering global scope across scripts. It does not permit a direct `eval` inside a function to introduce a `var` that conflicts with an existing lexical binding in that function."},"1703":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-9","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#compatibility","content":"Code that previously produced an early redeclaration error can now run, while the property created by `eval` remains observable through `globalThis`."},"1704":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-10","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Make eval-introduced global vars redeclarable § consequences"},"1705":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-11","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1706":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-12","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Make eval-introduced global vars redeclarable § proposal"},"1707":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-13","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1708":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-14","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Make eval-introduced global vars redeclarable § does-this-break-symmetry-with-function-scope-vars-and-direct-eval"},"1709":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-15","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1710":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-16","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2025: HasRestrictedGlobalProperty"},"1711":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-17","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2025-06-25, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1712":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-18","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"TC39 notes: redeclarable global eval vars for Stage 4"},"1713":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-19","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2025-02-18, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1714":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-20","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1715":{"id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars-21","page_id":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/redeclarable-global-eval-vars#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2025-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1716":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"page","content":"RegExp Buffer Boundaries","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ESNext"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries"},"1717":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-0","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#regexp-buffer-boundaries","content":"RegExp Buffer Boundaries"},"1718":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-1","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1719":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-2","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1720":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-3","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1721":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-4","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1722":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-5","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#regexp-buffer-boundaries","content":"`\\A`, `\\z`, and `\\Z` add zero-width assertions for the boundaries of the complete input buffer."},"1723":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-6","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#what-it-does","content":"`\\A` matches the start of the input and `\\z` matches its end, regardless of the multiline flag. `\\Z` matches before an optional final line-terminator sequence at the end. These forms correspond to patterns built from scoped RegExp modifiers."},"1724":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-7","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#gotchas","content":"The escapes require the `u` or `v` flag and are not valid inside a character class. Without Unicode mode, Annex B parses them as identity escapes for the literal letters."},"1725":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-8","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"TC39 active proposals at the cutoff"},"1726":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-9","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1727":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-10","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"Regular Expression Buffer Boundaries for ECMAScript § syntax"},"1728":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-11","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1729":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-12","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"Regular Expression Buffer Boundaries for ECMAScript § relationship-to-regexp-modifiers"},"1730":{"id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries-13","page_id":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-buffer-boundaries#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-23"},"1731":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"page","content":"RegExp dotAll flag","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2018"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag"},"1732":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-0","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#regexp-dotall-flag","content":"RegExp dotAll flag"},"1733":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-1","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1734":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-2","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1735":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-3","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1736":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-4","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#support","content":"Support"},"1737":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-5","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#related","content":"Related"},"1738":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-6","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1739":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-7","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#regexp-dotall-flag","content":"The `s` flag makes `.` match every character in a pattern, line terminators included."},"1740":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-8","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#what-it-does","content":"The `Atom :: .` production selects the set of all characters when `DotAll` is true and the set of all characters except `LineTerminator` otherwise. `RegExp.prototype.dotAll` is a getter that reports `true` when the receiver's `[[OriginalFlags]]` contains `s`, and `RegExp.prototype.flags` reads that accessor and appends `s` between `m` and `u`, so `/a/gimsuy.flags` is `\"gimsuy\"`."},"1741":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-9","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#what-it-does","content":"The proposal argues the old `.` was neither of the two things developers wanted: it excludes only four of the code points that can end a line, and it is most often written where \"any character\" is meant. It settles on `s` because engines with a flag for this mode already spell it that way, while `dotAll` names the accessor because the mode affects `.` and nothing else."},"1742":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-10","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#gotchas","content":"The flag widens `.` and nothing else, so `s` and `m` compose instead of conflicting: the proposal states that `multiline` mode affects only the anchors while `dotAll` mode affects only `.`. The four code points `.` used to reject are `\\n`, `\\r`, U+2028, and U+2029, so `/a.b/.test(\"a\\u2028b\")` is `false` while `/a.b/s.test(\"a\\u2028b\")` is `true` even though no `\\n` is involved. The accessor is on `RegExp.prototype`, so `RegExp.prototype.dotAll` itself reads as `undefined` rather than throwing, unlike the same getter applied to a non-regular-expression object."},"1743":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-11","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#support","content":"V8 enabled `s` by default in 6.2, whose example matches `/foo.bar/su` against a string containing a line feed."},"1744":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-12","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#related","content":"RegExp modifiers turn the same `s` flag on or off for one group instead of the whole pattern."},"1745":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-13","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: s (dotAll) flag for regular expressions"},"1746":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-14","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1747":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-15","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: s (dotAll) flag — Motivation"},"1748":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-16","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1749":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-17","page_id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag#sources","content":"ECMAScript proposal: s (dotAll) flag — How does dotAll mode affect multiline mode?"},"1750":{"id":"/features/regexp-dotall-flag-18","page_id":