t":"V8 release v6.4: ECMAScript language features"},"1936":{"id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes-33","page_id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes#sources","content":"Engine record, published 2017-12-19, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1937":{"id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes-34","page_id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes#sources","content":"Results of the 115th Ecma General Assembly held in Geneva, June 27, 2018"},"1938":{"id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes-35","page_id":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-unicode-property-escapes#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2018-06-27, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1939":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"page","content":"RegExp v flag","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2024"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag"},"1940":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-0","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#regexp-v-flag","content":"RegExp v flag"},"1941":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-1","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1942":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-2","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1943":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-3","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1944":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-4","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1945":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-5","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#regexp-v-flag","content":"The `v` flag adds Unicode-aware set operations and multi-code-point string properties to regular expressions."},"1946":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-6","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#what-it-does","content":"Unicode sets mode supports nested character classes, intersection with `&&`, subtraction with `--`, string literals through `\\q{}`, and Unicode properties of strings. It incorporates Unicode mode while defining revised case-insensitive complement behavior."},"1947":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-7","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#gotchas","content":"The `u` and `v` flags cannot be combined. Some punctuation that is accepted unescaped inside a `u` character class is reserved in `v` mode, so changing a flag can require escaping the class contents. The proposal identifies the patterns that become errors under `v`."},"1948":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-8","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"RegExp v flag proposal § summary"},"1949":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-9","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1950":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-10","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"RegExp v flag proposal § high-level-api"},"1951":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-11","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1952":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-12","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"RegExp v flag proposal § how-is-the-v-flag-different-from-the-u-flag"},"1953":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-13","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1954":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-14","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"ECMAScript 2024: RegExp patterns"},"1955":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-15","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"ECMA-262 specification, published 2024-06-26, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1956":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-16","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"TC39 notes: RegExp v flag for Stage 4"},"1957":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-17","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"Committee notes, published 2023-05-16, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"1958":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-18","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"Ecma International approves new standards"},"1959":{"id":"/features/regexp-v-flag-19","page_id":"/features/regexp-v-flag","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/regexp-v-flag#sources","content":"Ecma General Assembly record, published 2024-07-03, reviewed 2026-07-24"},"1960":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"page","content":"Relative indexing with .at()","breadcrumbs":["ecmascript","ES2022"],"tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at"},"1961":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-0","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#relative-indexing-with-at","content":"Relative indexing with .at()"},"1962":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-1","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"1963":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-2","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"1964":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-3","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"1965":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-4","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"1966":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-5","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#relative-indexing-with-at","content":"Arrays, strings, and typed arrays gain an `at(index)` method that counts backwards from the end when the index is negative."},"1967":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-6","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#what-it-does","content":"`Array.prototype.at` converts its argument with `ToIntegerOrInfinity`, adds the length when the result is negative, returns `undefined` for an out-of-range position, and otherwise performs an ordinary `Get`. `String.prototype.at` follows the same index arithmetic and returns the one-character substring at that position. The typed-array form validates the receiver first and reads `[[ArrayLength]]` instead of a `length` property. The method exists because indexing cannot be redefined: `arr[-1]` already reads a property literally named `-1`, so negative subscripts could not be given new meaning, and the previous workarounds either name the collection twice or allocate a throwaway array through `slice(-N)[0]`."},"1968":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-7","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#gotchas","content":"The index is truncated, not rounded, because `ToIntegerOrInfinity` drops the fractional part: `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"].at(1.9)` is `\"b\"` and `.at(-1.9)` resolves to index 2, giving `\"c\"`. The string form is code-unit based, so returning the substring from `k` to `k + 1` splits an astral character: `\"\\u{1F600}\".at(0)` is one half of a surrogate pair with `length` 1. An out-of-range index yields `undefined` rather than an error, so a missing element and a real `undefined` element look alike. The method almost did not ship under this name: the earlier `.item()` spelling broke sites that duck-typed DOM collections by the presence of an `item` property, and advancement waited on a marketplace site that used array instances as hashmaps with `at` as a key."},"1969":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-8","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content":"Proposal for an .at() method on all the built-in indexables § rationale"},"1970":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-9","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1971":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-10","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content":"Proposal for an .at() method on all the built-in indexables § existing-methods"},"1972":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-11","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"1973":{"id":"/features/relative-indexing-at-12","page_id":"/features/relative-indexing-at","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/relative-indexing-at#sources","content