features/shared-array-buffer-0","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sharedarraybuffer","content":"SharedArrayBuffer"},"2027":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-1","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#what-it-does","content":"What it does"},"2028":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-2","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#before--after","content":"Before / after"},"2029":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-3","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#gotchas","content":"Gotchas"},"2030":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-4","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#support","content":"Support"},"2031":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-5","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#related","content":"Related"},"2032":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-6","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sources","content":"Primary sources"},"2033":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-7","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sharedarraybuffer","content":"A buffer whose bytes stay shared when it is passed to another agent, so writes are visible to every holder."},"2034":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-8","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#what-it-does","content":"`new SharedArrayBuffer(length)` allocates a Shared Data Block through `AllocateSharedArrayBuffer` and throws a `TypeError` when called without `new`. The clause specifies a `byteLength` accessor, an `@@species` getter that returns the receiver, and a prototype tagged `SharedArrayBuffer`. Instances are never detached, which distinguishes them from `ArrayBuffer` instances. `slice` resolves a species constructor, rejects a result that reuses the same Shared Data Block, and copies the selected range into it, and the same typed-array views that work over an `ArrayBuffer` work over this buffer."},"2035":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-9","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#what-it-does","content":"Shared bytes only matter because the specification now has more than one agent: an agent owns its execution contexts and job queues but its Agent Record carries a `[[CanBlock]]` flag, and agents in one cluster may share an executing thread only when none of them can block. The proposal's tutorial describes the intended flow: allocate the buffer in one agent, hand it to another with the host's message-passing call, and both sides then hold the same memory."},"2036":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-10","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#gotchas","content":"Sharing the bytes does not make plain reads and writes ordered. The tutorial states that a program which writes an element in one agent and reads it in another without synchronization is not well defined, and that the reader can observe values other than the old one or the new one. Ordering comes from the `Atomics` operations, not from the buffer."},"2037":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-11","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#gotchas","content":"`slice` looks like aliasing but is not: it allocates a new buffer through the species constructor and rejects a result that shares the source's Shared Data Block, so a view over the slice does not see later writes to the source. Allocation is also not free at any granularity: the tutorial reports that one implementation rounded shared objects up to multiples of 4 KiB with a minimum of 8 KiB, on the assumption that programs allocate a few large buffers and partition them with views."},"2038":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-12","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#support","content":"V8 shipped the constructor in 6.0, which presents it as the mechanism for sharing memory between workers and names porting threaded applications through asm.js or WebAssembly as a case it unlocks."},"2039":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-13","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#related","content":"atomics"},"2040":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-14","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#related","content":"resizable-arraybuffers"},"2041":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-15","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#related","content":"ts-generic-typedarrays"},"2042":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-16","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sources","content":"ecmascript\\_sharedmem: Shared Memory and Atomics proposal"},"2043":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-17","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sources","content":"TC39 proposal repository, reviewed 2026-07-26"},"2044":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-18","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sources","content":"Shared memory tutorial: Allocating shared memory, and sharing it"},"2045":{"id":"/features/shared-array-buffer-19","page_id":"/features/shared-array-buffer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/features/shared-array-buffer#sources","content