6":1549,"3977":1548,"3978":1547,"3979":1546,"3980":1462,"3981":3663,"3982":3662,"3983":3661,"3984":2564,"3985":1567,"3986":1747},"orderedDocs":[[1390,"\"Own\" is literal: an inherited property answers `false`, so `Object.hasOwn(Object.create({ a: 1 }), \"a\")` reports nothing even though `\"a\" in target` would be `true`. Because the target passes through `ToObject`, a primitive is boxed instead of rejected and `Object.hasOwn(\"ab\", 1)` is `true`, while `null` and `undefined` throw a `TypeError`. Array holes count as absent, so `Object.hasOwn([1, , 3], 1)` is `false` while index `1` still reads as `undefined`. The key is a property key, not a string comparison: `Object.hasOwn({ \"1\": true }, 1)` is `true`."],[3549,"`--checkers` controls type-checker workers, while `--builders` controls concurrent project-reference builders under build mode. `--singleThreaded` constrains checking, parsing, and emit to one thread. Type-checker work is divided deterministically for a fixed worker count."],[1284,"`??` evaluates its right operand only when the left one is `null` or `undefined`, leaving other falsy values in place."],[1502,"`?.` yields `undefined` when the value to its left is `null` or `undefined`, and skips the rest of the chain that follows it."],[1794,"`(?<=…)` and `(?<!…)` test what precedes the current position without adding it to the match."],[701,"`**=` joins the `AssignmentOperator` list next to `*=` and `+=`. The same edition redefines `Math.pow` to return the result of applying the `**` operator, so the method and the operator share one algorithm rather than two independently specified ones. The proposal argues for the infix form as being shorter than function notation and cites the operator's use in mathematics, physics, and robotics."],[3108,"`/** @import { SomeType } from \"pkg\" */` introduces a type-only name for later JSDoc annotations. Namespace imports are also supported, and neither form emits a runtime import."],[3433,"`/// <reference no-default-lib=\"true\"/>` no longer excludes default libraries from the program. The supported controls are the `noLib` compiler option and explicit library replacement."],[1723,"`\\A` matches the start of the input and `\\z` matches its end, regardless of the multiline flag. `\\Z` matches before an optional final line-terminator sequence at the end. These forms correspond to patterns built from scoped RegExp modifiers."],[1722,"`\\A`, `\\z`, and `\\Z` add zero-width assertions for the boundaries of the complete input buffer."],[1910,"`\\p{…}` and `\\P{…}` match code points by their Unicode character-database properties inside a Unicode-mode pattern."],[1152,"`&&=`, `||=`, and `??=` write to their target only when the left operand passes the test their logical operator applies."],[365,"`+` is the one arithmetic operator that accepts a mixed pair, and only with a string: `1n + \"2\"` produces `\"12\"` while `1n + 1` throws. Unary `+` is rejected on a BigInt, because it reaches `ToNumber`, whose BigInt row throws a `TypeError`; the same row is why every `Math` method rejects one. Serialization fails rather than degrading: `SerializeJSONProperty` throws a `TypeError` for a BigInt value, so a payload holding one needs a `toJSON` method or a replacer, and that clause does consult `toJSON` on a BigInt. Converting a large `Number` literal loses digits before `BigInt` ever sees them, so `BigInt(9007199254740993)` yields `9007199254740992n` while the string form `BigInt(\"9007199254740993\")` is exact. `0n` is falsy, so a guard written as `if (count)` treats an explicit zero the same way it treats a missing value."],[2627,"`${configDir}` resolves to the directory containing the configuration file in which it is written."],[9,"`AggregateError` is a built-in error whose own `errors` property carries the list of individual failures one operation collected."],[93,"`Array.fromAsync` collects async iterables, sync iterables, or array-like inputs into a promised array."],[1967,"`Array.prototype.at` converts its argument with `ToIntegerOrInfinity`, adds the length when the result is negative, returns `undefined` for an out-of-range position, and otherwise performs an ordinary `Get`. `String.prototype.at` follows the same index arithmetic and returns the one-character substring at that position. The typed-array form validates the receiver first and reads `[[ArrayLength]]` instead of a `length` property. The method exists because indexing cannot be redefined: `arr[-1]` already reads a property literally named `-1`, so negative subscripts could not be given new meaning, and the previous workarounds either name the collection twice or allocate a throwaway array through `slice(-N)[0]`."],[134,"`Array.prototype.includes(searchElement, fromIndex)` walks indices in ascending order, compares each element to `searchElement` with SameValueZero, and returns `true` at the first match or `false` after the last index. `fromIndex` defaults to 0, counts from the end when negative, clamps to 0 when the computed index is below 0, and yields `false` without a search when it reaches the length. SameValueZero differs from strict equality by treating `NaN` as equal to itself, and from `SameValue` by treating `+0` and `-0` as equal."],[1795,"`Assertion` gains the alternatives `(?<= Disjunction )` and `(?<! Disjunction )` beside the existing lookahead forms. Both evaluate their `Disjunction` with a direction argument of `-1` rather than `+1`, so the inner pattern consumes input from the assertion's position towards the start of the string; the positive form keeps the captures its `Disjunction` made, and the negative form succeeds only when the inner match fails."],[196,"`async` is not a reserved word and `await` is only an operator inside an async body. The declaration form requires `async` with no line terminator before `function`, so a newline between them parses `async` as an identifier reference instead. `function f() { var await = 1; }` stays legal, while the same body inside `async function` is a `SyntaxError`, and `const async = 5` is still a valid declaration."],[1871,"`Atom` accepts `( GroupSpecifier Disjunction )`, and `AtomEscape` accepts `\\k GroupName` when the pattern's `[+N]` parameter is set. `RegExpBuiltinExec` creates a `groups` property on every result array: an `ObjectCreate(null)` when the pattern contains any `GroupName` and `undefined` when it does not, then stores each named capture under its `RegExpIdentifierName` alongside the numbered slot. `GetSubstitution` recognises `$<`, scans to the next `>`, and substitutes `Get(namedCaptures, groupName)` or the empty string when that capture is `undefined`."],[268,"`Atomics.pause` supplies an implementation-defined, finite CPU pause for spin-wait loops."],[313,"`Atomics.wait` puts the calling agent into a wait queue for a cell and sleeps until it is woken or the timeout elapses, returning `\"not-equal\"`, `\"timed-out\"`, or `\"ok\"`, and `Atomics.wake` releases up to a requested count of agents queued on that cell. `Atomics.isLockFree` reports, per datum size, whether an atomic step needs a lock outside the datum, and always returns `true` for size 4. A separate memory model clause constrains the orderings these events may take, states that a race-free program behaves as an interleaving of its agents, and states that no ordering is left undefined."],[315,"`Atomics.wait` reports why it stopped instead of throwing, and one of its outcomes needs no other agent at all: a value argument that does not match the cell returns `\"not-equal\"` immediately, and a finite timeout that elapses returns `\"timed-out\"`. Only `Int32Array` is accepted by `wait` and `wake`, because both validate with `onlyInt32` set, and `wait` throws a `TypeError` when the surrounding agent cannot block. The tutorial states that blocking is permitted on a browser's main thread but that hosts are expected to deny it there."],[289,"`Atomics.waitAsync()` waits for a shared-memory notification without blocking the calling agent."],[312,"`Atomics` is a single ordinary object, not callable and not constructable, whose functions operate indivisibly on shared memory cells and let agents wait for and dispatch events. `add`, `sub`, `and`, `or`, `xor`, and `exchange` all route through `AtomicReadModifyWrite`, which loads a value, combines it with the argument, stores the result, and returns the value it loaded. The tutorial tabulates the same set together with `load`, `store`, and `compareExchange`, and states that every read-modify-write member returns the previous cell value."],[2277,"`CanBeHeldWeakly` decides which values a weak collection accepts: every object, plus every symbol whose `KeyForSymbol` result is undefined. That single operation covers `WeakMap` keys, `WeakSet` elements, a `WeakRef` target, and both the target and the unregister token of a `FinalizationRegistry`, and `WeakMap.prototype.set` throws a `TypeError` when `CanBeHeldWeakly(key)` is false. The proposal frames the change as removing a restriction that forced object identity onto keys."],[1911,"`CharacterClassEscape` gains `p{ UnicodePropertyValueExpression }` and `P{ … }`, both guarded by the `[+U]` parameter, and the expression is either `UnicodePropertyName=UnicodePropertyValue` or a lone name or value. `\\p{…}` returns the code points whose database definition carries that property and value, and `\\P{…}` returns the complement. `UnicodeMatchProperty` resolves a name or alias against two fixed tables and requires implementations to reject every other spelling, so `Script_Extensions` and `scx` are valid while `script_extensions` and `Scx` are not."],[2107,"`ClassElement` admits `static MethodDefinition` and `static FieldDefinition ;`, and the name of either may be a `PrivateIdentifier`. Class definition evaluation adds every static private method to the constructor `F` and then walks the static elements in source order, calling `DefineField(F, …)` for each static field. `this` inside a static field initializer is that constructor, so `static x = this.name` reads the class name. The three class-features proposals were presented together, and this one's repository now records only the merge into class fields."],[459,"`ClassElement` gains a `ClassStaticBlock` production, `static { ClassStaticBlockStatementList }`. Its body becomes a zero-parameter function created in the surrounding lexical and private environments, with the class as its home object. Class definition evaluation puts static blocks and static fields into one `staticElements` list and processes them in source order, calling the block's body function with the constructor as its `this` value. The proposal targets initialization that a single field initializer cannot express, such as a `try`/`catch` or setting two static fields from one computed value, and sharing a private name with a function declared in the same scope."],[1285,"`CoalesceExpression` takes a `CoalesceExpressionHead` on the left and a `BitwiseORExpression` on the right, and it sits beside `LogicalORExpression` under `ShortCircuitExpression`; the head may itself be a `CoalesceExpression` or a `BitwiseORExpression`, and nothing more. That shape means no production accepts an unparenthesized `&&` or `||` next to `??`. Evaluation returns the left value unless it is `undefined` or `null`, in which case the right operand is evaluated and returned; `&&` and `||` instead branch on `ToBoolean` of the left value. The proposal's own list of the cases `||` gets wrong is a header string of `\"\"`, an animation duration of `0`, and a `false` flag, each of which `||` replaces with the default."],[1110,"`DoubleStringCharacter` and `SingleStringCharacter` each gain `<LS>` and `<PS>` alternatives beside `SourceCharacter but not one of \" or \\ or LineTerminator`, and the clause's note now lists only the closing quote, U+005C, U+000D, and U+000A as code points that cannot appear literally. The proposal states the mismatch this removes: JSON strings may carry those two code points unescaped while ECMAScript string literals could not, so `JSON.parse` claimed a subset relationship the grammar did not support. It also records that the restriction stays in regular expression literals, because those are not part of JSON, and that template literals already accepted both code points."],[792,"`EnumerateObjectProperties` gains a requirement: unless the object or something in its prototype chain is a Proxy, an integer-indexed, a module namespace, or a host-provided exotic object, its iterator must behave as `CreateForInIterator(O)` would, and the requirement lapses once a `[[Prototype]]` changes, a property is removed, a property is added to a prototype, or an `[[Enumerable]]` attribute changes. The named iterator collects the string keys of `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, yields those whose descriptor is enumerable while recording every key it inspected, then repeats on the prototype and skips keys already recorded. Own keys therefore arrive in array-index order first, then string keys in property creation order. The same clause carries an informative generator function that reproduces the required behavior. The proposal names the other APIs that expose the same own-property order: `Object.keys`, `Object.values`, `Object.entries`, `JSON.parse` through `InternalizeJSONProperty`, and `JSON.stringify` through `SerializeJSONObject`. Those go through `EnumerableOwnPropertyNames`, which walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]` in list order and keeps only enumerable string keys, so they share the own-property sequence without walking the prototype chain."],[791,"`EnumerateObjectProperties` must now match a specified For-In Iterator for ordinary objects, so `for`-`in` order stops being implementation-defined in the common case."],[2753,"`erasableSyntaxOnly` reports TypeScript constructs that require JavaScript generation rather than type erasure."],[595,"`Error ( message [ , options ] )` calls `InstallErrorCause` after setting `message`. That operation checks `HasProperty(options, \"cause\")` and, when present, copies the value with `CreateNonEnumerableDataPropertyOrThrow`. The resulting property is writable and configurable but not enumerable. The same step appears in every `NativeError` constructor and in `AggregateError`, which runs it before collecting its `errors` list. The proposal's stated aim is a single spelling that tooling can rely on, replacing per-codebase conventions for attaching the original error. It also draws a line against `AggregateError`: `cause` accumulates depth along one failure path, while an aggregate collects unrelated errors caught in one place."],[628,"`Error.isError` performs an internal brand check for native error objects."],[662,"`Error.prototype.stack` becomes a specified accessor while the stack string remains implementation-defined."],[2789,"`es2025` is accepted for both `target` and `lib`, with declarations for that annual ECMAScript library."],[866,"`eval` parses with the `Script` goal and accepts a hashbang, while the `Function` constructor builds a `FunctionBody` and rejects one, so `new Function(\"#!/usr/bin/env node\\nreturn 1\")` throws a `SyntaxError`. A byte-order mark placed ahead of `#!` also stops the line from being read as a hashbang, though browsers remove that mark while fetching an external script or module, before the text reaches the parser."],[42,"`findLast()` calls its predicate in descending index order and returns the matching element, or `undefined` when no call returns a truthy value. `findLastIndex()` returns the index of that element and `-1` when nothing matches. Both accept an optional `thisArg` and both are also installed on typed arrays, where `%TypedArray%.prototype.findLast` validates the receiver as a typed array before the search."],[68,"`flat(undefined)` flattens one level while `flat(null)` and `flat(0)` only copy, because the depth default applies to an absent or `undefined` argument and every other value goes through `ToInteger`. Only values for which `IsArray` is true are spliced, so `[[1], \"ab\", new Set([2])].flat()` keeps the string and the set intact. `flatMap` always flattens exactly one level and ignores any depth argument, so a mapper returning `[[value]]` yields nested arrays. Holes vanish rather than becoming `undefined`, since the walk skips an index whose `HasProperty` check fails. Both names are listed in `@@unscopables`, so a `with` block over an array does not resolve `flat` or `flatMap` to the methods."],[66,"`flat` copies an array with nested arrays spliced in to a chosen depth, and `flatMap` maps each element before splicing one level."],[67,"`flat` reads `length`, sets its depth to `1` unless the argument is present, in which case it takes `ToInteger(depth)`, creates the result with `ArraySpeciesCreate(O, 0)`, and delegates to `FlattenIntoArray`. `flatMap` throws a `TypeError` when its first argument is not callable and calls the same operation with a fixed depth of `1` plus the mapper and `thisArg`. `FlattenIntoArray` visits each index, skips one whose `HasProperty` check fails, applies the mapper when present, and recurses only while the remaining depth is above zero and `IsArray` holds for the element; otherwise it writes the element with `CreateDataPropertyOrThrow` and throws a `TypeError` past index 2^53 − 1. `Array.prototype[@@unscopables]` gains `flat` and `flatMap` entries."],[773,"`Float16Array` adds a two-byte floating-point typed array. `DataView.prototype.getFloat16` and `setFloat16` access binary16 fields with explicit byte order, while `Math.f16round` returns the closest representable binary16 value as a Number."],[231,"`for await` accepts a synchronous iterable too, and then awaits each yielded value: `for await (const value of [Promise.resolve(\"a\"), \"b\"])` produces `\"a\"` and `\"b\"` rather than a promise and a string, because the statement falls back to an Async-from-Sync Iterator object. That fallback makes the loop silently accept an operand that publishes only `Symbol.iterator`."],[679,"`for await` controls asynchronous iteration and does not imply asynchronous disposal; `await using` is still required for that behavior. A value other than `null` or `undefined` must provide the required disposal method or initialization throws."],[816,"`Function.prototype.toString` returns `func.[[SourceText]]` whenever the receiver has that slot holding a String and `HostHasSourceTextAvailable(func)` is true; a bound function exotic object or a built-in function object instead yields an implementation-defined string with the syntax of `NativeFunction`, and for a well-known intrinsic that is not an anonymous function the `PropertyName` part of that string must be the initial value of its `name` property. Each function definition form sets `[[SourceText]]` to the source text matched by its own production, so the slot holds the characters as written rather than a normalised rendering. `CreateDynamicFunction` has no matched source text, so it synthesises one: the kind's prefix, `\" anonymous(\"`, the joined parameter string, a LINE FEED, `\") {\"`, a LINE FEED, the body text, a LINE FEED, and `\"}\"`."],[815,"`Function.prototype.toString` returns the exact source text a function was defined from, or `NativeFunction` syntax when no such text exists."],[3193,"`getOrInsert` accepts a fallback value and returns the stored or inserted value. `getOrInsertComputed` accepts a callback that receives the key and runs only when the key is absent."],[1176,"`getOrInsert` takes a ready default value. `getOrInsertComputed` calls its callback with the missing key and stores the returned value only when no entry exists. Both forms are available on `Map` and `WeakMap`."],[841,"`globalThis` names the global `this` value through one writable, configurable, non-enumerable property of the global object."],[524,"`HostGetCodeForEval` lets a host extract source from an object that the host recognizes as code-like. The compilation guard receives the original arguments, the assembled source, and the compilation kind for `eval` and dynamic function constructors. Existing non-string values keep the early-return behavior unless a host recognizes them."],[2988,"`ignoreDeprecations: \"6.0\"` suppresses the general assertion diagnostic, but NodeNext-family modes can reject old syntax independently according to their runtime model."],[3008,"`import defer * as feature from \"./feature.js\"` binds a typed module namespace. The imported module evaluates when a property of that namespace is first accessed, so its top-level statements and side effects run after the importing module has started. The compiler rejects named and default bindings with this import phase. It preserves the syntax in output rather than downleveling deferred evaluation."],[484,"`import defer * as namespace from \"./module.js\"` produces a module namespace exotic object. Reading one of its properties synchronously triggers evaluation if the module has not run. Loading still prepares the complete dependency graph and populates the module cache. The proposed semantics separate loading from evaluation."],[483,"`import defer` loads and links a module graph while postponing synchronous evaluation until namespace access."],[2075,"`import source moduleSource from \"./module.js\"` binds a module-source object, and `import.source(\"./module.js\")` provides the dynamic form. The static form accepts a default binding only. A host-defined source object has `AbstractModuleSource.prototype` in its prototype chain and retains the underlying module source record."],[947,"`import text from \"./message.txt\" with { type: \"text\" }` binds the decoded contents as the default import. The proposal does not add an encoding option. A caller that needs a specific encoding instead imports bytes and decodes them explicitly."],[914,"`import.meta` gives each module one host-populated object for metadata such as its own location."],[540,"`import(specifier)` evaluates a runtime expression as a module specifier and returns a promise for the resulting module namespace object."],[541,"`ImportCall` takes one `AssignmentExpression`, applies `ToString` to its value, and returns the promise of a capability created before the host is asked to load anything; a failing coercion rejects that promise instead of throwing. The host performs the load and reports the outcome by calling `FinishDynamicImport`, which resolves the promise with `GetModuleNamespace` of the requested record or rejects it with the failure. The host hook accepts a null referencing script or module, which is what allows the form outside any module. The proposal lists the differences from an `import` declaration: it is usable from a script, it is not hoisted, it accepts a computed string, and its presence creates no dependency that must be fetched before the containing module evaluates."],[2570,"`isolatedDeclarations` continues to reject the source form. Emitted declarations that preserve the entity name are not guaranteed to parse or check under earlier compiler releases."],[3041,"`isolatedDeclarations` requires exported declarations to carry enough type information for single-file declaration emit."],[3955,"`isWellFormed()` returns false when a string contains an unpaired or out-of-order surrogate code unit. `toWellFormed()` returns a string in which each such code unit is replaced by `U+FFFD`; already well-formed content is preserved."],[229,"`IterationStatement` gains `for await ( … of AssignmentExpression ) Statement` beside the `for-in` and `for-of` forms. `Symbol.asyncIterator` is the well-known symbol an object publishes to advertise the protocol, non-writable and non-configurable like the other well-known symbols. An AsyncIterator's `next` returns a promise for an iterator result, and once a returned promise fulfils with `done: true` every later call must do the same. `async function*` declarations, methods, and expressions produce async generators, and an Async-from-Sync Iterator object adapts a plain synchronous iterator when the operand publishes no `Symbol.asyncIterator`."],[977,"`Iterator.concat` lazily yields the contents of multiple iterable inputs in sequence."],[962,"`Iterator.prototype.chunks(size)` lazily consumes up to `size` values for each result array and retains a shorter last chunk. `Iterator.prototype.windows(size)` advances by one value and emits full overlapping windows by default. Its second argument accepts `\"allow-partial\"` to emit a shorter terminal window."],[1015,"`Iterator.prototype.includes` consumes an iterator until it finds a matching value or reaches exhaustion."],[1031,"`Iterator.prototype.join` converts yielded values to strings and concatenates them with a separator."],[1050,"`Iterator.zip` takes an iterable of iterables and yields arrays. `Iterator.zipKeyed` takes an object of iterables and yields objects with the same keys. Their `mode` option stops at the shortest input, pads to the longest input, or requires equal lengths."],[3075,"`IteratorObject` extends `Iterator` and returns itself from `[Symbol.iterator]()`. Built-in array, map, set, and generator iterators use specialized subtypes and expose the ECMAScript iterator helper methods. `AsyncIteratorObject` supplies the parallel type shape without a runtime constructor."],[3931,"`JSON.stringify` emits a `\\uXXXX` escape for every unpaired surrogate code unit instead of copying it into the result."],[3139,"`libReplacement` controls whether default library declarations can be resolved from `@typescript/lib-*` packages."],[996,"`map`, `filter`, `take`, `drop`, and `flatMap` return lazy iterator helpers. `reduce`, `toArray`, `forEach`, `some`, `every`, and `find` consume the iterator, while `Iterator.from` wraps iterator-like inputs."],[2135,"`matchAll` reads the `flags` property of its argument, throws a `TypeError` when the string form contains no `\"g\"`, and otherwise calls the argument's `@@matchAll` method; a non-regular-expression argument is wrapped by `RegExpCreate(regexp, \"g\")` first. `RegExp.prototype[@@matchAll]` builds a fresh matcher through `SpeciesConstructor`, copies the receiver's `lastIndex` onto it, and hands that copy to `CreateRegExpStringIterator`. Each `next` call runs `RegExpExec` on the copy, so iteration walks the copy's `lastIndex` and never writes to the original object. Every iteration result value is the same array `exec` produces, with its capture groups, `index`, and `input`. The stated reason for returning an iterator rather than an array is that gathering every match of a large string or of many capture groups has a cost the caller may not want, and a spread or `Array.from` recovers the array."],[2134,"`matchAll` returns an iterator of full match arrays and leaves the `lastIndex` of the regular expression it was given untouched."],[1197,"`Math.sumPrecise` returns the correctly rounded sum of an iterable of Number values."],[2164,"`maxLength` is a cap, not a minimum: a target no greater than the current length returns the string unchanged, so `\"abcdef\".padStart(3, \"0\")` is `\"abcdef\"` and a negative or `NaN` target leaves `\"7\"` alone. An empty `fillString` also short-circuits, so `\"ab\".padStart(5, \"\")` stays `\"ab\"` instead of being space-padded."],[3122,"`module Foo {}` must be written as `namespace Foo {}`. String-named ambient declarations such as `declare module \"package\"` remain supported because they describe external modules rather than internal namespaces."],[3312,"`module: \"node18\"` pairs the corresponding Node module-resolution behavior with a non-floating ruleset. It allows import assertions and rejects CommonJS `require()` calls targeting ECMAScript modules. The release notes contrast its behavior with `nodenext`."],[3327,"`module: \"node20\"` permits compatible CommonJS `require()` calls to ECMAScript modules and rejects import assertions. Unless configured separately, it implies an `es2023` target. The release notes contrast these rules and defaults with NodeNext."],[3341,"`module: preserve` retains each ECMAScript import or export while emitting TypeScript import assignments as `require()` calls."],[2499,"`moduleResolution: \"bundler\"` can be paired with `module: \"commonjs\"`."],[739,"`new FinalizationRegistry(cleanupCallback)` requires `new` and a callable argument, then stores that callback with an empty cell list and the realm of the constructor. `register(target, heldValue, unregisterToken)` appends a cell recording all three, rejecting a non-object target, a token that is neither an object nor `undefined`, and a `heldValue` that is the same value as the target. `unregister(token)` removes every cell whose recorded token matches and reports whether anything was removed. `CleanupFinalizationRegistry` states that an implementation *may* remove a cell whose target has been cleared and call the callback with that cell's held value. Scheduling belongs to the host, which is expected to make that call later without interrupting synchronous execution. The proposal's motivating case is a weak cache whose map entries would otherwise accumulate after their values are collected."],[2034,"`new SharedArrayBuffer(length)` allocates a Shared Data Block through `AllocateSharedArrayBuffer` and throws a `TypeError` when called without `new`. The clause specifies a `byteLength` accessor, an `@@species` getter that returns the receiver, and a prototype tagged `SharedArrayBuffer`. Instances are never detached, which distinguishes them from `ArrayBuffer` instances. `slice` resolves a species constructor, rejects a result that reuses the same Shared Data Block, and copies the selected range into it, and the same typed-array views that work over an `ArrayBuffer` work over this buffer."],[3896,"`new WeakRef(target)` requires `new`, rejects a non-object target, records it in `[[WeakRefTarget]]`, and passes it to `AddToKeptObjects`. `deref` reads that slot and returns the target, or `undefined` when the reference has been cleared. The kept-a