 rules out a leading, trailing, or doubled separator, and the `0` form of `DecimalIntegerLiteral` has no continuation, so a separator cannot follow a lone leading zero. Annex B's legacy octal and legacy-octal-like productions take no `[Sep]` parameter, so the old `0`-prefixed forms stay separator-free. The proposal's stated aim is reading long digit runs, including fixed-point amounts whose scale is otherwise invisible. It also records the deliberately restrictive rule: consecutive separators and separators next to the ends of a literal were left out because no practical use was found."],[1340,"`Object.fromEntries` builds a plain object from an iterable of key-value pairs, inverting `Object.entries`."],[1341,"`Object.fromEntries` calls `RequireObjectCoercible` on its argument, creates an ordinary object with `%ObjectPrototype%`, asserts that the new object is extensible and has no own properties, and hands `AddEntriesFromIterable` an adder built from the `CreateDataPropertyOnObject` steps. That adder defines each pair with `CreateDataPropertyOrThrow`, which writes a writable, enumerable, configurable own property through `[[DefineOwnProperty]]` rather than through an assignment. The proposal names the gap it closes: an object could already be turned into a `Map` with `new Map(Object.entries(obj))`, but the reverse direction needed a helper or an inline reducer."],[114,"`Object.groupBy()` converts callback results to property keys, while `Map.groupBy()` does not. Consumers must account for absent groups because only keys produced by the callback appear in the result. The specification creates groups only as callback keys are encountered."],[2918,"`Object.groupBy()` produces optional properties because a callback may omit any possible key. `Map.groupBy()` preserves arbitrary key types in a `Map`. The declarations initially require `esnext` library types."],[113,"`Object.groupBy()` returns a null-prototype object whose property keys map to arrays. `Map.groupBy()` returns a `Map` and preserves arbitrary key values by identity. Both pass each element and its zero-based index to the callback."],[1388,"`Object.hasOwn(target, key)` reports an own property without routing the call through the target's own prototype chain."],[1389,"`Object.hasOwn` coerces its first argument with `ToObject`, its second with `ToPropertyKey`, and returns the result of `HasOwnProperty`. `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty` performs the same two conversions in the opposite order, and the champion described that reordering as the whole functional difference, observable only in which conversion throws first. The two cases the proposal targets are objects that never inherit the method and objects that shadow it: `Object.create(null)` produces a target on which `hasOwnProperty` is not a function, and an own `hasOwnProperty` member can make the call do something else entirely."],[1391,"`Object.hasOwn` shipped in V8 9.3, which that release describes as an easier-to-reach alias for `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call`."],[1451,"`Object.values` coerces its argument with `ToObject` and returns `EnumerableOwnProperties(obj, \"value\")` as an array, and `Object.entries` does the same with the kind `\"key+value\"`, so each element is a two-element array. That abstract operation walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, keeps only keys whose type is String and whose descriptor is enumerable, reads each value with `Get`, and orders the result the way `EnumerateObjectProperties` would."],[1415,"`ObjectBindingPattern` gains a `BindingRestProperty` alternative, both alone and after a property list. `RestDestructuringAssignmentEvaluation` creates an object with `%ObjectPrototype%` and copies into it every property except the names the pattern already bound. The proposal frames the pair as the object counterparts of array rest elements and array spread elements, and names the rest form's job as collecting the remaining own enumerable keys the pattern did not pick off."],[2162,"`padStart` coerces the receiver with `RequireObjectCoercible` and `ToString`, converts `maxLength` with `ToLength`, returns the string unchanged when that target is not greater than the current length, repeats `fillString` to exactly the missing length, and concatenates the filler before the string. `padEnd` performs the same steps and concatenates the filler after the string instead. The default `fillString` is a single U+0020 SPACE in both methods."],[1560,"`Promise.allKeyed` and `Promise.allSettledKeyed` preserve dictionary keys while combining promise results."],[1561,"`Promise.allKeyed` waits for the values of an object's own enumerable properties and fulfills with an object using the same keys. `Promise.allSettledKeyed` produces keyed fulfillment or rejection records. Enumerable symbol keys participate alongside string keys."],[1580,"`Promise.allSettled` creates a promise capability from its receiver, iterates the argument, and closes the iterator when the collection step fails. The collection step reads `resolve` from the constructor once, throws a `TypeError` when it is not callable, appends `undefined` to the result list for each input, and attaches a resolve and a reject element function for that index; the outer promise resolves only when the shared remaining count reaches zero. A fulfilled input stores an object with `status` set to `\"fulfilled\"` and a `value` property, and a rejected input stores `status` set to `\"rejected\"` with a `reason` property, each written at its own index. The proposal's stated distinction from the other combinators is that this one never short-circuits and always waits for all of its input values."],[1579,"`Promise.allSettled` waits for every input promise to settle and fulfils with one outcome record per input."],[1609,"`Promise.any` resolves each iterated value through its constructor's `resolve` and returns a promise that the first fulfilment settles. `PerformPromiseAny` keeps one `errors` list plus a remaining-elements counter, appending an `undefined` placeholder per input and attaching the capability's own resolve function as `onFulfilled`. Each rejection runs a reject element function that writes the reason into its reserved index, decrements the counter, and rejects the outer promise with a new `AggregateError` when the counter reaches zero. The proposal places the combinator opposite `Promise.all`, which short-circuits on the first rejection instead of the first fulfilment."],[1608,"`Promise.any` settles as soon as one input promise fulfils, and rejects with an `AggregateError` only when every input has rejected."],[1667,"`Promise.resolve(callback())` does not capture an exception thrown before `Promise.resolve` is called. `Promise.try` performs the call inside its promise-producing operation."],[1665,"`Promise.try` invokes a callback and represents its return value or thrown exception with a promise."],[1682,"`Promise.withResolvers()` returns a promise together with its resolve and reject functions."],[3932,"`QuoteJSONString` walks the value as UTF-16 encoded code points and now escapes a code point with `UnicodeEscape` when it has the same numeric value as a leading or a trailing surrogate, alongside the existing rule for code points below U+0020. A well-formed surrogate pair decodes to a supplementary code point, so it takes the final branch and is copied through unchanged. The proposal states the problem this removes: the result claimed to be UTF-16 while an isolated code unit in D800–DFFF is ill-formed, which left no UTF-8 representation for the returned text."],[1772,"`RegExp.escape` converts a string into text that a regular-expression pattern matches literally."],[1827,"`RegExpBuiltinExec` sets an internal `hasIndices` from the presence of `d` in the pattern's original flags, and only then adds an `indices` property to the result array. That value is an array of `[start, end]` pairs aligned with the numbered captures, carrying a `groups` property that repeats the pairs under each named group. `RegExp.prototype.hasIndices` is a getter that reports whether the flag is set. The proposal names syntax highlighting as the driving case, where the position of an individual capture matters and not only the position of the whole match, and `d` was picked for the `d` in \"indices\", aligning with `index` and `lastIndex`."],[568,"`RelationalExpression` gains the production `PrivateIdentifier in ShiftExpression`. Evaluating it resolves the identifier in the running private environment, requires the right operand to be an Object, and returns `true` when `PrivateElementFind` locates a matching entry. That lookup only inspects the object's own `[[PrivateElements]]` list. The check covers all three private kinds, and the champion presented it as reporting fields, private methods, and private accessors alike. The proposal's motivation is a check that neither throws nor discloses that a private field is the mechanism."],[2197,"`replaceAll` collects every match position with `StringIndexOf` before building the result, advancing by `max(1, searchLength)` after each hit. `replace` locates a single position and returns after one substitution. Both route a `searchValue` that carries `@@replace` to that method, but `replaceAll` first reads `flags` and throws a `TypeError` when the string form of `flags` contains no `\"g\"`. The proposal ties that rejection to the method name: a missing global flag asks for one replacement while the name asks for all of them. A string replacement still passes through `GetSubstitution`, so `$&`, `` $` ``, `$'`, and `$$` keep their meaning in each individual replacement, and a function replacement is called once per match with the matched text, its position, and the whole string."],[2196,"`replaceAll` substitutes every occurrence of a string search value and rejects a regular expression that has no global flag."],[3633,"`rewriteRelativeImportExtensions` changes TypeScript file extensions in relative imports to their emitted JavaScript forms."],[2037,"`slice` looks like aliasing but is not: it allocates a new buffer through the species constructor and rejects a result that shares the source's Shared Data Block, so a view over the slice does not see later writes to the source. Allocation is also not free at any granularity: the tutorial reports that one implementation rounded shared objects up to multiples of 4 KiB with a minimum of 8 KiB, on the assumption that programs allocate a few large buffers and partition them with views."],[3681,"`stableTypeOrdering` applies deterministic content-based ordering compatible with the native compiler's output."],[3697,"`strictBuiltinIteratorReturn` gives built-in iterators an `undefined` return value instead of `any`."],[2225,"`String.prototype.trimStart` calls `TrimString(S, \"start\")` and `String.prototype.trimEnd` calls `TrimString(S, \"end\")`. `TrimString` applies `RequireObjectCoercible` and `ToString` to the receiver, then removes leading, trailing, or both runs according to `where`, and defines the removed set as the union of `WhiteSpace` and `LineTerminator`. Annex B keeps `trimLeft` and `trimRight` as properties whose initial values are the same function objects as `trimStart` and `trimEnd`, and recommends the new names in new code. The proposal gives the naming reason: symmetry with `padStart` and `padEnd`, with the older names retained for web compatibility."],[228,"`Symbol.asyncIterator`, `for await (… of …)`, and `async function*` together iterate a sequence whose completion is only known asynchronously."],[2252,"`Symbol.prototype.description` is an accessor property whose set accessor is `undefined`; its get accessor coerces the receiver with `thisSymbolValue` and returns that symbol's `[[Description]]`. `Symbol ( [ description ] )` leaves the slot `undefined` when the argument is absent and otherwise stores `ToString(description)`, so the slot distinguishes a missing argument from an empty string. `SymbolDescriptiveString`, which `Symbol.prototype.toString` calls, substitutes the empty string for an `undefined` description before wrapping it in `Symbol(` and `)`, so that route erases the distinction."],[2253,"`Symbol(\"\")` and `Symbol()` produce the same `toString()` text, `\"Symbol()\"`, so string slicing reports an empty description for both; only the getter separates `\"\"` from `undefined`. There is no setter, so `symbol.description = \"x\"` is discarded in sloppy code and throws a `TypeError` under strict mode, because the property's set accessor function is `undefined`. The getter reaches the same slot for every symbol, so a well-known symbol reports its specification name, `Symbol.iterator.description` being `\"Symbol.iterator\"`, and a symbol from `Symbol.for` reports its registry key rather than any wrapper text."],[2808,"`target: \"es5\"` is the deprecated lowest target, and specifying `downlevelIteration` at either boolean value is also deprecated because that option only affects ES5 emit. The diagnostics can be silenced with `ignoreDeprecations: \"6.0\"` while the stable compiler retains the behavior."],[2358,"`Temporal.Instant` represents a point on the time line. `Temporal.ZonedDateTime` combines an instant with a time zone and calendar, while the `Plain` types represent civil values without a time zone. `Temporal.Duration` represents a length of time for arithmetic and comparison with the required context."],[3873,"`toBase64` and `toHex` encode a byte view, while `Uint8Array.fromBase64` and `fromHex` allocate decoded bytes. `setFromBase64` and `setFromHex` decode into an existing view and report the characters read and bytes written. Base64 options select the standard or URL alphabet, padding output, and final-chunk handling."],[397,"`toReversed()`, `toSorted(comparefn)`, `toSpliced(start, skipCount, ...items)`, and `with(index, value)` allocate a result array of the required length and fill it from the receiver, which stays untouched. `toReversed` builds that array by reading each source