2, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[687,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[950,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[965,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1020,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1035,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1059,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1139,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1269,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1564,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[1726,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[2090,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[2370,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-23"],[280,"TC39 stage record, published 2026-07-22, reviewed 2026-07-26"],[2293,"Template literal revision"],[2294,"Template literal revision"],[3742,"Template literal types compare interpolated placeholder types with greater precision."],[3752,"Template literal Unicode code points"],[3753,"Template literal Unicode code points"],[2304,"Template Literals Revision"],[3735,"Template string interpolation checks"],[3736,"Template string interpolation checks"],[3759,"Template-literal inference preserves a Unicode code point instead of splitting its surrogate pair."],[2343,"temporal"],[3777,"temporal"],[2350,"Temporal"],[2351,"Temporal"],[2327,"Temporal is available by default in the listed engines, while other engines require separate compatibility checks."],[3767,"Temporal library declarations"],[3768,"Temporal library declarations"],[2359,"Temporal objects are immutable and do not replace or subclass `Date`. Calendar-day arithmetic and exact elapsed-time arithmetic are distinct operations, especially across time-zone offset transitions."],[2361,"Temporal proposal § documentation"],[2363,"Temporal proposal § principles"],[2371,"Temporal proposal specification"],[2320,"Temporal runtime availability"],[2321,"Temporal runtime availability"],[2357,"Temporal supplies immutable objects for exact time, civil dates and times, time zones, calendars, and durations."],[2360,"temporal-runtime-support"],[3778,"temporal-runtime-support"],[948,"Text import depends on the host's module loading support and is not equivalent to fetching a resource at runtime. A non-default text encoding requires the separate byte-import path described by the proposal."],[192,"The `async` keyword marks a function whose body can suspend on `await` and whose call returns a promise."],[1740,"The `Atom :: .` production selects the set of all characters when `DotAll` is true and the set of all characters except `LineTerminator` otherwise. `RegExp.prototype.dotAll` is a getter that reports `true` when the receiver's `[[OriginalFlags]]` contains `s`, and `RegExp.prototype.flags` reads that accessor and appends `s` between `m` and `u`, so `/a/gimsuy.flags` is `\"gimsuy\"`."],[3698,"The `BuiltinIteratorReturn` intrinsic resolves to `undefined` when the option is enabled and to `any` otherwise. The option is included by `strict`, so reading `value` before checking `done` can produce an error."],[1479,"The `Catch` production gains a second alternative, `catch Block`, alongside `catch ( CatchParameter ) Block`. `CatchClauseEvaluation` for that alternative returns the result of evaluating the `Block` directly, without creating a declarative environment record or running `BindingInitialization`, both of which the parameterised alternative still performs. The proposal names the patterns it targets: probing for an unimplemented web feature, setting a success flag, and parsing possibly malformed JSON with a fallback value, each of which writes a binding it never reads."],[1826,"The `d` flag makes a match result carry an `indices` array holding the start and end offset of the match and of every capture group."],[3192,"The `esnext` library declares `getOrInsert` and `getOrInsertComputed` on Map and WeakMap."],[2407,"The `FormalParameters` production gains an alternative that ends a `FormalParameterList` with a comma, and `Arguments` gains the form `( ArgumentList , )`. Both are grammar-only changes, so the same code parses in every place those productions are used: function declarations and expressions, arrow functions, methods, generators, class constructors, `new` expressions, and ordinary calls."],[1873,"The `groups` object has a `null` prototype, so `groups.toString` is `undefined` and `groups.hasOwnProperty` cannot be called; the proposal put the names on a separate object precisely to keep them from colliding with `length`, `index`, and `input` on the result array. The property exists whatever the pattern looks like: `RegExpBuiltinExec` always performs the `CreateDataProperty` step, so `\"groups\" in /\\d+/.exec(\"7\")` is `true` while the value is `undefined`."],[865,"The `HashbangComment :: #! SingleLineCommentChars?` production makes the line a comment that is discarded from the input elements handed to the syntactic grammar. It is recognized only through the `InputElementHashbangOrRegExp` goal symbol, and that goal is used at the start of a Script or Module; `#!` stays outside the grammar elsewhere so it cannot collide with an infix use of those punctuators, and `//` remains the way to comment a later line. Command-line hosts previously stripped the line themselves before handing valid source text to the engine."],[567,"The `in` operator takes a private name on its left side and answers whether the right operand carries that private element."],[3432,"The `no-default-lib` triple-slash reference is parsed but ignored."],[3357,"The `node` alias and `node10` value select the deprecated Node 10 strategy. `classic` is also deprecated. `baseUrl` remains usable but is deprecated as a general lookup root; `paths` mappings no longer require it as a prefix. The stable compiler checks all three settings."],[3311,"The `node18` module option provides fixed module checking and emission rules for the Node.js 18 model."],[3326,"The `node20` module option fixes module checking and resolution to the Node.js 20 model."],[1739,"The `s` flag makes `.` match every character in a pattern, line terminators included."],[2106,"The `static` modifier applies to a field declaration and to a `#`-named method, placing both on the constructor itself."],[946,"The `text` import attribute loads a referenced resource as a string."],[3376,"The `typescript` package now supplies the Go-based native compiler and does not expose a programmatic compiler API."],[1947,"The `u` and `v` flags cannot be combined. Some punctuation that is accepted unescaped inside a `u` character class is reserved in `v` mode, so changing a flag can require escaping the class contents. The proposal identifies the patterns that become errors under `v`."],[1945,"The `v` flag adds Unicode-aware set operations and multi-code-point string properties to regular expressions."],[705,"The 7th edition coerces both operands with `ToNumber`, so at this edition the operator has one numeric type. ECMAScript 2020 replaces those steps with `ToNumeric` and dispatches to the BigInt exponentiation operation when both operands are BigInts, which is what makes `2n ** 10n` evaluate to `1024n` and a mixed `2n ** 2` throw a `TypeError`."],[318,"The accepted buffer kind also widened. In this edition every member validates its argument with `ValidateSharedIntegerTypedArray`, which throws a `TypeError` unless the view's buffer is a `SharedArrayBuffer`, so `Atomics.load` over a plain `ArrayBuffer` was rejected here even though later editions accept it."],[2600,"The additional index signature changes structural assignability and emitted declaration shape for classes using non-literal computed methods. The release notes contrast the previous empty shape with the new signature."],[3825,"The affected calls are not described by a single source pattern; the release notes state that changed types and new errors depend on the inference graph."],[3807,"The API mirrors the single-file shape of `transpileModule` for `.d.ts` output. Accurate results require input that satisfies `isolatedDeclarations`, which supplies all types needed without following imports."],[302,"The Atomics object"],[303,"The Atomics object"],[843,"The binding is ordinary data, not a keyword: `globalThis.globalThis = 42` succeeds and every later read gives `42`, which follows from the writable and configurable attributes. Because the property is non-enumerable, `Object.keys(globalThis)` omits it, so a feature test has to use `typeof globalThis` or `\"globalThis\" in globalThis`. On the web the value is not the object that holds global variables: the proposal records that `globalThis` is the `WindowProxy`, which keeps its identity across navigation while the underlying `Window` is replaced, so a global variable can disappear from it. A top-level `this` is not a substitute either, since it is `undefined` in a module and in strict-mode function calls."],[485,"The binding must be a namespace binding. Modules with top-level `await`, plus the asynchronous portion of their transitive graph, are evaluated eagerly because namespace property access cannot pause for asynchronous evaluation."],[1219,"The built-in path performs no type checking and ignores `tsconfig.json`. It chooses CommonJS or ECMAScript-module behavior using the same package and extension rules as JavaScript, with `.mts` and `.cts` selecting fixed module systems. Imports retain explicit file extensions because Node does not rewrite specifiers."],[1639,"The callback receives nothing at all: `Promise.prototype.finally.length` is `1` while the argument count seen inside the callback is `0`, and the callback also runs on a rejected promise without marking it handled, so a `.finally` at the end of a chain still produces an unhandled rejection. When the callback is not callable, it is passed straight through to `then` in both positions, which makes `Promise.resolve(2).finally(null)` behave like `then(null, null)`."],[1666,"The callback runs synchronously. Its returned value is used to resolve the new promise, and a synchronous exception rejects that promise. Additional arguments after the callback are passed to the callback without creating a wrapper function."],[2534,"The change affects invocations that combine an existing `tsconfig.json` in the current directory with source-file arguments."],[1701,"The change removes the global environment record's special list of names introduced by `var` inside `eval`. `HasRestrictedGlobalProperty` now reports a restriction only for a non-configurable own property of the global object, so a later script can declare the same name with `let`, `const`, or `class` and shadow the configurable property on `globalThis`. The specification returns false for a configurable existing property, which is the revised rule the proposal requested."],[630,"The check identifies native error branding, not whether a value was thrown or contains particular fields. JavaScript permits any value to be thrown, so caught values still require separate handling when `Error.isError` returns false."],[2754,"The check rejects enums, parameter properties, runtime namespaces, `import =`, and `export =`. It accepts type annotations and other syntax that a stripping runtime can remove while leaving valid JavaScript."],[1504,"The check tests only the value in front of the operator, so `config.server?.log(\"x\")` still throws a `TypeError` when `server` exists and `log` is missing; guarding the call needs a second operator, as in `config.server?.log?.(\"x\")`. Wrapping a chain in parentheses restores the failure: `(empty.server?.port).toFixed(0)` throws because grouping limits the scope of short-circuiting. `delete empty.server?.port` reports `true` without touching anything, which the proposal presents as falling out of how `delete` treats a non-reference. Three neighbouring forms are rejected by the grammar rather than made optional: `new a?.()`, a tagged template after a chain, and `a?.b = 1`; the proposal lists them as deliberately unsupported so support could be added later. The digit lookahead means the token is context-sensitive: `flag?.3:0` evaluates the conditional and prints `0.3` when `flag` is truthy."],[3823,"The checker changes some inferred type arguments when earlier inference allowed a type variable to escape its intended relationship. A contextual type that refers to an outer type parameter is instantiated to its constraint before inferences are made to the return type of an inner call, so the outer parameter can no longer be inferred for itself. The pull request's `call(wrap(id), 1)` result changes from `A` to `unknown`."],[3563,"The checker finds the last assignment to a parameter or `let` variable and preserves a preceding narrowing in closures created after that point. An assignment from any nested function prevents the preservation because that write can occur later."],[2614,"The checker no longer substitutes a generic parameter's constraint into a conditional type and then picks one branch. When the constraint proves neither outcome, the constraint of `T extends Foo ? X : Y` becomes the union of both branches, so `IsArray<U>` for `U extends object` is assignable to neither `true` nor `false`."],[2856,"The checker rejects generic calls where contextual checking previously accepted an inconsistent function expression. Some affected calls need an explicit type argument to establish the intended instantiation."],[3401,"The checker remains permissive when a variable may have been assigned before the nested function runs. It errors when analysis finds no assignment anywhere, closing a gap caused by optimistic assumptions about callback timing."],[3243,"The code action updates the called function's parameter list from the extra arguments at a call site. It is available through editors that expose TypeScript code fixes."],[817,"The comment and the space before the parameter list survive, because the slot stores the matched characters, so any code that compares `fn.toString()` against a canonical spelling now sees the original formatting. A bound function keeps no source text: `tally.bind(null).toString()` returns `NativeFunction` syntax whose `PropertyName` is absent even though `tally.bind(null).name` is `\"bound tally\"`, since the bound-function branch is checked before the `[[SourceText]]` branch. A function built by the `Function` constructor reports the synthesised shape rather than the arguments as passed, so its text always contains three added line feeds. A host may return `false` from `HostHasSourceTextAvailable`, which drops that function to the `NativeFunction` result as well, and the operation must answer the same way every time it is called with the same function."],[2500,"The compiler applies bundler-oriented lookup rules while emitting CommonJS module syntax. This provides a migration route for configurations leaving the deprecated Node 10 resolution strategy without changing output format in the same step."],[3713,"The compiler checks every source file using JavaScript strict-mode syntax rules."],[3651,"The compiler no longer infers a common source directory for configured projects. Command-line compilation without a configuration file still infers the root. The change can add source-directory segments beneath `outDir`."],[3007,"The compiler parses and checks namespace imports that use the deferred module-evaluation syntax."],[2653,"The compiler rejects conditions whose syntax proves they are always truthy, falsy, or non-nullish."],[3605,"The compiler reports syntax, backreference, and target-version errors in regular expression literals."],[1178,"The computed callback is skipped when the key already exists, including when its stored value is `undefined`. WeakMap variants still require keys that can be held weakly."],[663,"The configurable, non-enumerable getter returns an implementation-defined stack string for an Error object and `undefined` for another object. The setter installs an own `stack` property on an Error object."],[1137,"The constructor properties are shared mutable state and can be overwritten by an unrelated successful match. The proposal places the entire feature in Annex B and does not make it a portable substitute for reading a match result."],[10,"The constructor takes `errors` and an optional `message`, converts the first argument with `IterableToList`, and installs the resulting array as a non-enumerable, writable, configurable own `errors` property. `IterableToList` walks the argument through its iterator protocol, so any iterable works and a non-iterable throws. The constructor is callable without `new`, in which case the active function object stands in for `NewTarget`. `AggregateError.prototype.name` is `\"AggregateError\"` and `AggregateError.prototype.message` is the empty string, so an omitted message leaves nothing on the instance. Instances carry the `[[ErrorData]]` slot that `Object.prototype.toString` uses to recognise an error. The proposal chose an error type over a plain array so that everything a built-in throws stays an `Error` instance and carries a stack trace."],[2136,"The copy inherits the receiver's position, so `pattern.lastIndex = 10` before the call skips everything before index 10 and the property still reads `10` afterwards. Two spreads of one result differ: the iterator sets its `[[Done]]` slot once `RegExpExec` returns `null` and then yields nothing further, so a second pass over the same iterator produces an empty array. A pattern that can match the empty string still terminates, because that clause advances `lastIndex` with `AdvanceStringIndex` after an empty match: `[...\"xx\".matchAll(/(?:)/g)]` yields matches at 0, 1, and 2. The rejection tests the observable `flags` string, so a `RegExp` subclass whose `flags` getter returns `\"y\"` is refused even when the instance was constructed with `\"g\"`."],[3667,"The declarations take another `Set` in every method, type `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, and `symmetricDifference` as producing a new `Set`, and type `isSubsetOf`, `isSupersetOf`, and `isDisjointFrom` as producing a `boolean`. No declaration mutates its receiver."],[1051,"The default mode stops when the first input finishes and closes unfinished iterators. Longest mode takes per-input padding values; strict mode throws when lengths differ."],[2550,"The defaults are `strict: true`, `module: \"esnext\"`, the latest supported annual ECMAScript target, `noUncheckedSideEffectImports: true`, and `libReplacement: false`. An explicit configuration value still overrides each default."],[3257,"The deprecated `module` values are `amd`, `umd`, `system`, and `none`; the AMD module-name directive has no continuing role. `outFile` is deprecated as the compiler's concatenating single-file output. The stable compiler checks these values together."],[3536,"The diagnostic appears only with `noImplicitAny` enabled and `strictNullChecks` disabled, so a project already using null-aware checking sees no change."],[2532,"The diagnostic prevents a filename invocation from silently ignoring the discovered configuration. The new `--ignoreConfig` flag makes that bypass explicit and compiles the named files with command-line defaults and options."],[2696,"The disabled set includes `target: ES3`, `charset`, legacy import-elision controls, several relaxed checking flags, `out`, `prepend`, and implicit operating-system-specific `newLine`. They can still appear in configuration before becoming configuration errors later."],[1480,"The earlier `catch ({})` workaround is not equivalent: destructuring a thrown `null` or `undefined` raises a `TypeError` from inside the handler, so the original exception is replaced. The proposal identifies that completion value as the one observable difference between the pattern and the new grammar, and the parameterless clause runs no `BindingInitialization`, so it cannot fail that way. The parentheses go with the parameter: `try {} catch () {}` is a `SyntaxError`, because the grammar offers only `catch ( CatchParameter ) Block` and `catch Block`."],[1177,"The earlier `Map.prototype.emplace` design used insert and update callbacks. It was replaced by the get-or-insert API, so both names belong to this page rather than separate catalogue entries."],[3293,"The enabled interop behavior becomes the supported direction for CommonJS consumption from ECMAScript modules. The stable compiler retains false settings only through the suppressible deprecation path. The release notes describe the import behavior, and the tag records suppression. The stable compiler checks both false values."],[3124,"The error applies to TypeScript's old internal-module alias, not to the `module` compiler option or ambient external-module declarations."],[2807,"The ES5 target and `downlevelIteration` option produce deprecation diagnostics."],[3933,"The escape appears in the serialized text only; the string being serialized still holds the lone surrogate, so a length or index check on the value is unaffected. `JSON.parse` turns the six-character escape back into the same code unit, so the round trip is lossless and a consumer that already accepted the ill-formed output sees an equivalent value. Paired surrogates are still emitted as the raw supplementary character rather than as two escapes, so only ill-formed input changes shape. The escape is a reverse solidus, `u`, and the code unit's value as a four-digit lowercase hexadecimal number padded with zeroes, which matters to code that compares serialized text byte for byte."],[1913,"The escapes exist only under `u`, because the grammar productions carry `[+U]`. Without that flag `\\p` stays an identity escape for the letter `p`, so `/^\\p{Letter}+$/` matches the nine-character string `p{Letter}` and not `p`. The proposal explains that ECMAScript 2015 already made `\\p` throw under `u`, which is what left the syntax free to redefine."],[1724,"The escapes require the `u` or `v` flag and are not valid inside a character class. Without Unicode mode, Annex B parses them as identity escapes for the literal letters."],[1684,"The exposed functions settle only their paired promise. Calling either function after settlement has no further effect, following ordinary promise resolution rules. The returned functions come from the standard promise capability algorithms."],[2087,"The first version reads a WebAssembly module through the source phase, and the second drops the `--experimental-wasm-modules` requirement."],[3328,"The fixed mode does not acquire future NodeNext behavior and does not imply the floating `esnext` target. Projects need `nodenext` when their runtime contract follows later Node module semantics."],[3313,"The fixed mode does not acquire later NodeNext behavior. Projects that need Node's synchronous ECMAScript-module loading rules use the floating `nodenext` mode instead."],[1742,"The flag widens `.` and nothing else, so `s` and `m` compose instead of conflicting: the proposal states that `multiline` mode affects only the anchors while `dotAll` mode affects only `.`. The four code points `.` used to reject are `\\n`, `\\r`, U+2028, and U+2029, so `/a.b/.test(\"a\\u2028b\")` is `false` while `/a.b/s.test(\"a\\u2028b\")` is `true` even though no `\\n` is involved. The accessor is on `RegExp.prototype`, so `RegExp.prototype.dotAll` itself reads as `undefined` rather than throwing, unlike the same getter applied to a non-regular-expression object."],[772,"The Float16 APIs store, read, write, and round numbers using IEEE 754 binary16 values."],[1851,"The form `(?ims-ims:subexpression)` changes the `i`, `m`, and `s` flags only within its group. Letters before `-` enable flags and letters after it disable flags, with no flag appearing twice."],[569,"The form is not total: the right operand must be an Object or the expression throws a `TypeError`, so `#amount in 5` throws where a `try`-based check would have returned `false`, and a guard against primitives still has to run first. A `Proxy` wrapping an instance reports `false`, because the lookup reads the `[[PrivateElements]]` of the object handed to it and a proxy has its own empty list; the same holds for `Object.create(Instance.prototype)`, which never ran instance initialization. The left operand is a literal private name, not a value: the proposal notes that this choice rules out any later shorthand where a bare `#x` would mean `this.#x`."],[3226,"The generated configuration uses NodeNext modules, an `esnext` target, an empty `types` array, source maps, declarations, declaration maps, and selected strictness options. It keeps a grouped handful of commented options and a link to the TSConfig reference in place of the full option catalogue. The earlier output turned on six settings and left every other option commented out beside its description. The 5.8 baseline sets `target: \"es2016\"`, `module: \"commonjs\"`, `esModuleInterop`, `forceConsistentCasingInFileNames`, `strict`, and `skipLibCheck`."],[193,"The grammar adds `AsyncFunctionDeclaration`, `AsyncFunctionExpression`, and `AsyncMethod`, each written as `async` followed with no line break by `function` or by a property name. Calling one creates a promise capability before the body runs, starts the body, and returns that capability's promise, so a `return` fulfills it and a thrown exception rejects it. `await` resolves its operand through a new promise capability and registers resumption functions that restore the saved execution context, which is how the suspended body continues with the fulfillment value or resumes with a throw completion."],[700,"The grammar adds `ExponentiationExpression`, whose second production is `UpdateExpression ** ExponentiationExpression`. The right operand is itself an `ExponentiationExpression`, which makes the operator right-associative, and the left operand is an `UpdateExpression`, which excludes a unary operator there. Evaluation applies `ToNumber` to both operand values and then the abstract operation \"Applying the `**` Operator\". That operation returns an implementation-dependent approximation of the base raised to the exponent, and fixes the results for zero, one, infinite, and `NaN` operands."],[112,"The grouping methods partition iterable values by keys returned from a callback."],[1968,"The index is truncated, not rounded, because `ToIntegerOrInfinity` drops the fractional part: `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"].at(1.9)` is `\"b\"` and `.at(-1.9)` resolves to index 2, giving `\"c\"`. The string form is code-unit based, so returning the substring from `k` to `k + 1` splits an astral character: `\"\\u{1F600}\".at(0)` is one half of a surrogate pair with `length` 1. An out-of-range index yields `undefined` rather than an error, so a missing element and a real `undefined` element look alike. The method almost did not ship under this name: the earlier `.item()` spelling broke sites that duck-typed DOM collections by the presence of an `item` property, and advancement waited on a marketplace site that used array instances as hashmaps with `at` as a key."],[2969,"The language server accepts a maximum length for quick-info text and uses a larger default limit."],[3619,"The language service accepts a relevant source range and returns its diagnostics promptly, followed by whole-file diagnostics. The checker also stabilizes message construction across cached and uncached paths."],[2889,"The language service can distinguish whether punctuation such as `.` or `,` safely commits a particular completion. Editors that consume this metadata avoid suppressing all commit characters in ambiguous syntax positions."],[3728,"The language service can suggest `#`-prefixed internal package specifiers without duplicating those mappings under `paths`. The containing package's `imports` field remains the resolution authority."],[2471,"The language service filters auto-import suggestions with configured module-specifier patterns."],[2839,"The language service preview lets an editor request deeper or shallower quick-info type displays."],[2708,"The language service searches farther through configuration ancestry while bounding referenced composite-project probes."],[2300,"The lexical grammar gains `TemplateCharacter :: \\ NotEscapeSequence`, covering a backslash followed by text that is not a legal escape sequence. The early error for that production is now conditional: it is a Syntax Error only when the `[Tagged]` parameter was not set, so an untagged template still rejects the sequence while a tagged one parses. The Template Value of `\\ NotEscapeSequence` is undefined, and an undefined component makes the whole component's Template Value undefined, while the Template Raw Value keeps the escape interpreted literally. `GetTemplateObject` builds the cooked array from the Template Values and the `raw` array from the Template Raw Values, so the tag receives `undefined` in one and the source text in the other."],[2790,"The library includes `RegExp.escape` and moves declarations such as `Promise.try`, iterator helpers, and Set methods from `esnext` into the annual library. The target does not add a new syntax transform because the annual edition adds no new JavaScript syntax."],[2770,"The library includes resizable buffer types, grouping methods, `Promise.withResolvers`, and other edition APIs. `Atomics.waitAsync` moves from `es2022` into the edition-aligned library."],[3158,"The limitation is integration-specific: command-line checking can use the native compiler while editor support remains on the compatibility service."],[2957,"The mapping remains package-internal and follows package `imports` rules; it is not a `paths` alias available to arbitrary consumers. The release notes identify the feature as Node subpath imports configured by the package `imports` field."],[1365,"The method coerces its argument with `ToObject`, walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]` in list order, converts each descriptor with `FromPropertyDescriptor`, and defines the result as an own data property of a fresh plain object. `FromPropertyDescriptor` produces the `value`, `writable`, `get`, `set`, `enumerable`, and `configurable` fields that the descriptor actually has, and returns `undefined` for an absent descriptor. Symbol keys and non-enumerable keys are both included, and the result is shaped for direct use as the second argument of `Object.create` or `Object.defineProperties`."],[1016,"The method compares values with SameValueZero, matching the equality used by `Array.prototype.includes`. An optional nonnegative count skips that many yielded values before comparisons begin. A match closes the iterator and returns `true`; exhaustion returns `false`."],[1032,"The method consumes its receiver without first collecting the values into an array. The default separator is a comma. `null` and `undefined` values contribute empty strings, matching the corresponding array operation."],[1017,"The method consumes the receiver. Negative skip counts and finite counts above `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` throw a `RangeError`; non-integral counts throw a `TypeError`."],[1683,"The method creates the same capability supplied to a promise executor, but exposes `{ promise, resolve, reject }` without assigning outer variables from the executor callback. It invokes the constructor used as its receiver, so promise subclasses receive their own instances."],[1636,"The method reads the species constructor of the receiver, builds a `Then Finally` function and a `Catch Finally` function that both close over the callback, and calls `then` with the pair. The `Then Finally` function invokes the callback with no arguments, resolves its result, and then returns a thunk producing the original fulfillment value. The `Catch Finally` function does the same and then rethrows the original rejection reason."],[290,"The method returns `{ async, value }`. A failed initial comparison or immediate timeout returns a string synchronously; an actual wait returns a promise that fulfills with `\"ok\"` or `\"timed-out\"`. `Atomics.notify()` wakes synchronous and asynchronous waiters through the same wait queue."],[94,"The method returns a promise and consumes an async iterator when available. For a sync iterable, it awaits each yielded value; for an array-like input, it awaits each indexed value. An optional mapping callback runs on each awaited value before insertion."],[978,"The method validates that each argument is iterable when called, then opens and consumes each iterator only when iteration reaches it. Arrays or other one-element iterables can place immediate values between longer sequences."],[3342,"The mode implies `moduleResolution: bundler`, `esModuleInterop`, and `resolveJsonModule`. Import syntax selects the corresponding `import` or `require` condition during package resolution, independent of the containing file extension."],[3027,"The narrower result can reject later writes that were accepted when the same expression inferred a broader array type. The release notes show filtering that removes `undefined` from the result type."],[3548,"The native compiler parallelizes parsing, type checking, emit, and project-reference builds with configurable worker counts."],[3388,"The native compiler uses a rebuilt cross-platform file-watching implementation for `--watch`."],[3155,"The native language server uses the Language Server Protocol and lacks integrations that require the earlier programmatic API."],[916,"The object has a null prototype, so `import.meta.hasOwnProperty` is not a function and `Object.prototype` helpers have to be called through `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call`. It is writable and shared for the life of the module record: the same object is returned on every access, which the proposal accepts on the grounds that the object is local to the module and can only be shared by passing it around, so one part of a module can see a property another part added. Which properties exist is a host decision, because the specification's own default is an empty list: code that reads `import.meta.url` depends on the host, not on the language. The form is refused at parse time in a script, so it cannot be used to detect whether code runs as a module."],[1414,"The object initializer grammar gains `PropertyDefinition : ... AssignmentExpression`. Evaluating that production calls `CopyDataProperties` on the object under construction with an empty exclusion list. `CopyDataProperties` walks `[[OwnPropertyKeys]]`, skips any key whose own descriptor is not enumerable, and installs the rest with `CreateDataProperty`; a `source` of `undefined` or `null` leaves the target untouched."],[1198,"The operation accumulates as though using exact arithmetic and then converts the result to a Number, avoiding order-sensitive loss from repeated binary64 addition. It accepts an iterable rather than variadic arguments and rejects non-Number elements."],[3682,"The option changes ordering of unions, properties, displayed types, and declaration output that otherwise depends on internal allocation order. It lets a project compare output and inference behavior with the native compiler before switching compilers."],[3418,"The option does not establish that a program is type-correct. Run a separate `noEmit` check, and use different `tsBuildInfoFile` paths when the check and emit phases run concurrently."],[3683,"The option is intended for migration diagnosis rather than permanent use and can increase checking time. An ordering-triggered inference error commonly requires an explicit type argument or variable annotation."],[3042,"The option reports exports whose declaration types require cross-file inference. Explicit annotations and supported syntactic inference let declaration tools emit files without constructing a whole-program type graph."],[3043,"The option requires `declaration` or `composite` and does not change JavaScript emit. It checks whether isolated declaration generation is safe; it is not itself a separate emitter."],[291,"The outer result is not itself a promise. Callers must inspect `async` before awaiting `value`. Argument validation still throws synchronously, and the fulfillment promise does not reject."],[3377,"The package installs the native `tsc` executable for command-line checking and emit. Tools that import compiler functions cannot use this release as a drop-in API replacement. The compatibility package `@typescript/typescript6` exposes `tsc6` and re-exports the earlier JavaScript API for side-by-side use."],[2682,"The parser follows the narrowed ECMAScript decorator grammar, so `@super.decorate` on a class member is an error while `@(super.decorate)` parses."],[270,"The pause duration is not observable except through timing, and the call does not transfer execution to another thread. An earlier duration parameter was removed because implementations did not vary behavior from it."],[2933,"The preceding compiler's deprecations become hard errors or no-op settings without a suppression escape hatch."],[842,"The property's initial value is the `[[GlobalThisValue]]` of the realm's global environment record, and its attributes are writable, non-enumerable, and configurable. Setting up a realm passes a separate `thisValue` alongside the global object and stores it in that slot, defaulting to the global object when the host supplies nothing. One spelling therefore reaches the same value in a browser, a server runtime, and an engine shell. The proposal's own case against the workarounds is concrete: the list of identifiers to probe grows with every environment, and `Function('return this')()` is refused under some content security policies. The name was chosen after attempts to ship the property as `global` broke existing sites, and it deliberately says \"global `this` value\" rather than \"global object\"."],[1741,"The proposal argues the old `.` was neither of the two things developers wanted: it excludes only four of the code points that can end a line, and it is most often written where \"any character\" is meant. It settles on `s` because engines with a flag for this mode already spell it that way, while `dotAll` names the accessor because the mode affects `.` and nothing else."],[664,"The proposal does not standardize stack-frame syntax, file paths, line formatting, capture timing, or security redaction. Calling the getter with a primitive receiver throws a `TypeError`."],[2163,"The proposal gives column formatting of monospaced text as the case that decided the argument's meaning: `maxLength` caps the result, and minimum-length behavior was left to `String.prototype.repeat`. Padding takes the first part of a multi-character fill string, following Ruby and PHP, so that `string.padStart(mask.length, mask)` lines a value up under its mask. The proposal cites repeated per-application padding helpers as the reason for a built-in. Its earlier `padLeft` and `padRight` names were replaced at the November 2015 meeting, which chose `padStart` and `padEnd` and moved `trimLeft` and `trimRight` to matching names."],[1637,"The proposal names cleanup as the case it serves: hiding a spinner, closing file handles, or logging completion, where the outcome itself does not matter. It lists four differences from `then(f, f)`: one callback expression instead of two, no argument passed to the callback, a fulfillment value that survives, and a rejection reason that survives."],[1452,"The proposal names the target cases: obtaining own values when an object is used as a hash, and obtaining pairs for iteration, serialization, or feeding `new Map`, whose constructor already accepts an iterable of entries. It returns arrays rather than iterators to match `Object.keys`, and records that an iterator form would instead have been `Reflect.ownValues` and `Reflect.ownEntries`."],[1135,"The proposal specifies legacy constructor properties and `RegExp.prototype.compile` for web compatibility."],[195,"The proposal states its purpose as replacing the promise-and-generator model of asynchronous code with a language-level one."],[1796,"The proposal takes matching a currency amount without the sign as its case, `/(?<=\\$)\\d+(\\.\\d*)?/` returning `10.53` for `$10.53` and not matching `€10.53`, and states that any pattern is allowed inside the assertion, including an unbounded repetition."],[1366,"The proposal targets copying: `Object.assign` reads through `[[Get]]` and writes through `[[Set]]`, which turns an accessor into a plain value on the target and discards attributes, so composing prototypes and mixins whose methods are non-enumerable needed hand-written descriptor collection. The proposal's own examples are a shallow clone through `Object.create` and a merge through `Object.defineProperties`."],[230,"The proposal's argument is that a synchronous iterator must know both the next value and the done state by the time `next` returns, and that an iterator of promises does not fix this because only the value becomes asynchronous. It therefore returns one promise for the whole `{ value, done }` pair, and queues method calls internally so a second `next` before the first settles still resolves in order. Async generators differ from generators in three named ways: their `next`, `throw`, and `return` return promises, `await` and `for await` are allowed in the body, and `yield*` delegates to async iterables."],[2408,"The proposal's motivation is version-control attribution: with a trailing comma already present, appending a parameter or an argument on its own line stops rewriting the previous line and stops reassigning that line's blame to the person who added the comma. It also states that the change touches no semantics, so a trailing comma leaves a function's `length` alone."],[1872,"The proposal's stated problem is that a numbered group carries no indication of what it captured, so reordering the groups forces every reference to be renumbered. It also adds a trailing `groups` argument to a replacer function, and chose `$<name>` over `${name}` in replacement strings to mirror the group declaration and stay visually distinct from template literals."],[135,"The proposal's stated targets are the `indexOf(el) !== -1` idiom, which asks for a position in order to answer a membership question, and that idiom's failure on `NaN`. It keeps `fromIndex` for consistency with `Array.prototype.indexOf` and `String.prototype.includes`. The same proposal installs the method on `%TypedArray%.prototype`, as it does for every non-mutating array method, and `%TypedArray%.prototype.includes` runs the same algorithm against the `[[ArrayLength]]` slot instead of a `length` property read, after `ValidateTypedArray` rejects a receiver that is not a typed array."],[1912,"The proposal's target is the maintenance cost of hand-written ranges: a Unicode-aware class had to be generated at build time, shipped as pattern text, and regenerated whenever the Unicode Standard changed. With the escapes, an engine data update reaches existing patterns with no code change, and the lone-value form doubles as shorthand for `General_Category`, so `\\p{Letter}` means `\\p{General_Category=Letter}`."],[2303,"The raw array is unaffected either way, which is why a tag written against `strings.raw` needs no change. Each `raw` element keeps escape sequences uninterpreted, so `\\unicode` arrives as the eight characters of source text rather than as a decoded code point."],[1033,"The receiver is exhausted even though no array is allocated. If converting the separator or an element to a string throws, the method closes the underlying iterator."],[2014,"The receiver must be an actual `Set`, but the argument uses the set-like protocol. Result ordering follows each method's specified iteration rules rather than sorting values."],[3469,"The rejection is specific to NodeNext checking. The fixed Node 18 mode continues to permit import assertions."],[2226,"The removed set includes line terminators, so `\"\\u2028x\".trimStart()` drops the leading paragraph-class separator rather than stopping at the first non-space character. It also includes every code point in Unicode general category Space\\_Separator, so the leading U+00A0 above disappears from `trimStart` while `trimEnd` keeps it. The aliases are the same function objects, so `String.prototype.trimRight === String.prototype.trimEnd` holds and the proposal records the visible consequence: `String.prototype.trimRight.name` reads `\"trimEnd\"`, which breaks code that identifies a method by its `name`."],[1774,"The result is pattern source, not a complete `RegExp`, and it is not an inverse for the specification operation that serializes an existing pattern."],[1367,"The returned map is not a faithful copy of attributes at its own level. Every entry is created with `CreateDataProperty`, so a descriptor describing a non-writable, non-configurable property is itself stored as a writable, enumerable, configurable property of the result."],[1581,"The returned promise has no rejection path of its own, so a `.catch` attached to it never runs for an input failure and an unread `status` field silently hides one. Each record carries one payload key: a fulfilled entry has no `reason` and a rejected entry has no `value`, so reading `results[i].value` on a failure yields `undefined` rather than an error. Positions come from the input index, not the settle order, so the slowest input keeps its place in the array. An empty iterable fulfils with an empty array, because the remaining count starts at one and is decremented as soon as iteration ends. A thenable that calls both of its callbacks is recorded once: that same step creates one `alreadyCalled` record and installs it on the resolve and the reject element function of the index, so `{ then(resolve, reject) { reject(error); resolve(value); } }` produces a single `\"rejected\"` entry."],[3635,"The rewrite does not resolve `paths`, `baseUrl`, package exports, or package imports. Workspace packages that execute source and publish output need runtime conditions whose targets already use the correct extensions."],[3504,"The runtime still rejects a target graph that contains top-level `await`. The stable `node18` module mode retains the earlier prohibition on requiring ECMAScript modules."],[43,"The search reads every index in range rather than skipping absent ones, so a hole reaches the predicate as `undefined`: `[\"a\", , \"c\"].findLast((value) => value === undefined)` returns `undefined` after visiting index 1. FindViaPredicate gets each index and calls the predicate on the retrieved value."],[1311,"The separator belongs to source text, not to numeric parsing, so `StringNumericLiteral` uses the digit productions with separators disabled and `Number(\"1_000\")` is `NaN`, while `parseInt` reads the longest prefix its grammar accepts and `parseInt(\"1_000\", 10)` returns `1`: user input carrying separators has to be stripped before conversion. A BigInt cannot separate its suffix, so `1_000n` is valid and `1_n` is a `SyntaxError`, which the proposal spells out for the `n` suffix. Digits inside a Unicode code point escape are not numeric literals, so `\"\\u{1_F600}\"` is a `SyntaxError`; the Stage 4 discussion settled that question against allowing underscores there."],[2904,"The special handling applies to conditional expressions directly returned from functions with declared return types. It does not remove the general assignability behavior of `any` elsewhere."],[629,"The specification checks the `[[ErrorData]]` internal slot. The method returns true for `Error`, native error subclasses, and `AggregateError` instances, including instances created in another realm. It returns false for primitives and objects that only imitate an error's properties or string tag. The proposal explains why `instanceof` and `Object.prototype.toString` are insufficient brand checks."],[2841,"The stable compiler contains the feature as a preview, so an editor is not required to expose the controls. It changes displayed type detail, not the inferred type. The release notes label the feature as a preview for the compiler and editor partners."],[3359,"The stable compiler still runs these options when `ignoreDeprecations: \"6.0\"` suppresses their diagnostics. This resolves the handbook sentence that labels Classic resolution as removed. The stable tag's deprecation code keeps the option in the suppressible branch."],[3259,"The stable tag retains these options behind suppressible deprecation diagnostics even though the handbook describes some as unsupported or removed. The compiler's shipped deprecation branch is authoritative for stable behavior."],[2917,"The standard library declares the ECMAScript grouping methods for compatible library targets."],[232,"The statement is restricted to contexts that can await: the grammar places `for await` in the `IterationStatement` production, and the proposal states that async `for-of` is allowed only inside async functions and async generator functions. Iteration is also strictly sequential: the proposal states that each access to the next value implicitly awaits the promise the iterator method returned, so an async generator over network calls issues them one at a time."],[1773,"The static method escapes regular-expression syntax, punctuators, whitespace, line terminators, and lone surrogates. It also hex-escapes an initial ASCII letter or digit so concatenating the result after an escape cannot change how the pattern parses."],[1154,"The three operators disagree about falsy values that are not nullish: `counters.hits ||= 10` overwrites a stored `0`, while `counters.hits ??= 10` leaves it, because only `??=` tests for `undefined` and `null`. A skipped write also skips its errors: on a frozen object `config.retries ||= 5` is silent because the left value is truthy, while `config.label ||= \"default\"` on a falsy frozen property throws a `TypeError`, which follows the same clause's note that the error comes from the assignment step. Adding an anonymous function through `handler ||= function () {}` gives it the name `handler`, so a stack trace or `fn.name` check changes when the equivalent `handler = handler || function () {}` is rewritten. The saved write is observable through anything that watches the property, including a `Proxy` `set` trap, not only through an accessor."],[314,"The tutorial names the case that motivates the read-modify-write members: two agents incrementing one counter with a plain `++` can each load, add, and store around the other, losing an update. It names the case for the queue members as replacing a spin loop over an atomic load with sleeping until the writer signals the cell."],[2934,"The unsupported set covers ES5 targeting, downlevel iteration, Node 10 and Classic resolution, legacy module outputs, `baseUrl`, disabled module interop, disabled strictness, legacy namespace and import-assertion syntax, the removed default-library directive, and filename builds that bypass a discovered configuration. `ignoreDeprecations` cannot restore these behaviors."],[2301,"The V8 release notes give the use the relaxation enables: a tag that consumes another language's escape conventions, illustrated with a LaTeX processor whose input contains `\\newcommand` and `\\u{h}ere`."],[317,"The waking function was renamed after this edition. ECMAScript 2017 specifies it as `Atomics.wake`, while ECMAScript 2019 specifies the same operation as `Atomics.notify`, which is the name the example above calls."],[3389,"The watcher is a Go port derived from Parcel's watcher architecture with small platform assembly shims. It separates detecting filesystem changes from the compiler's decision about what to watch and why."],[3519,"The wrapped type still participates in assignability after another position determines the type argument. This lets an API identify